\par\par \b \uc2 JBIC to use Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves for dlr procurement\b0\par\par\uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2008\par 12:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 13 (Reuters) - Japan will start allowing the Japan Bank for International Cooperation (JBIC) to exchange its yen funds for dollars from the nation's foreign reserves for some of its dollar-denominated loans, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The move will reduce the size of Japan's reserves, the world's second-largest at around $1 trillion, albeit marginally. The amount of dollars JBIC can procure from Japan's foreign reserves is expected to total less than 500 billion yen ($4.82 billion) in the fiscal year to next March 31.\par \par \uc2 Finance ministry officials said the step was aimed at giving JBIC more diversified and effective ways of procuring dollar funds for its loans.\par \par \uc2 JBIC, a government-backed lender that provides loans linked to overseas aid and overseas project finance for Japanese firms, now trades with private-sector banks when it needs to procure dollars to make any dollar-denominated loans. It can also issue dollar-denominated bonds.\par \par \uc2 But starting this month, it will also be able to procure dollars from Japan's foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 While the ministry officials said the step was not aimed at reducing the reserves, some academics and analysts say Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have become too big.\par \par \uc2 One problem is that the government has to issue financing bills to balance a special budget account that holds Japan's foreign reserves when the asset side of the balance sheet increases, at a time when Japan needs to tackle a mountain of public debt.\par \par \uc2 A ministry official said the step could help reduce issuance of such bills.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounting to a record 20 trillion yen ($192.7 billion) in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004, as the government waged a campaign to prevent a rapid rise in the yen from derailing Japan's then-fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo has stayed out of the market since then, but Japan's reserves have grown slowly over the past few years on interest rate income from deposits and securities held in the reserves.\par \par \uc2 Japan began using dollars from its foreign reserves for overseas remittances last April. Before the policy change, the Bank of Japan had delivered cash in yen to private financial institutions on behalf of the government and paid for converting the money into the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The step reduced the size of reserves slightly, although the ministry has said the step was aimed at cutting government costs and not at cutting the reserves. ($1=103.76 Yen) (Reporting by Yoko Nishikawa, Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/RESERVES|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080513e45d0013u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
