\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Most big  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  firms see no  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  past 95 yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Akiko Takeda   \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2008\par 05:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Click on [nT196195] for main story and [T237065] for table)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 17 (Reuters) - Most major Japanese companies do not expect authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciates beyond 95 yen to the dollar, as such a move would have only a limited impact, a Reuters survey showed. Of 220 respondents, 61 percent don't expect the Japanese authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market while 39 percent think it may, according to the Reuters Tankan survey conducted from March 27 to April 14.\par \par \uc2 Asked if they hoped for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise beyond 95 yen to the dollar, the respondents were divided. Thirty-two percent of the 215 respondents to the question said they hoped for such action, 33 percent said they don't, and 35 percent said they had no preference.\par \par \uc2 Many respondents were sceptical of the effect of solo Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations should be left to the market. "Since it is one-sided dollar weakness and not yen strength, there is no meaning to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It's hard to see any effective result from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone," said a retail company official.\par \par \uc2 "It may be meaningful if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at easing sharp moves, but the effect would be extremely limited if it is done by Japan alone," said an electronic company official.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to 95.77 yen <JPY=> earlier in March, the lowest since August 1995, and was trading at 101.88 yen on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Of those who expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 40 percent think the authorities will step in when the yen trades between 90 to 92.5 yen to the dollar, followed by 31 percent who see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between 87.5 to 90 yen and 21 percent between 92.5 to 95 yen. Of the manufacturers who hoped for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , processing manufacturers such as food, electronics or precision machinery, whose export value would increase on a weak yen, had a higher proportion at 41 percent than 33 percent for basic materials industries that would see import costs rise from a weak yen.\par \par \uc2 "Due to fragile domestic demand, a slump in export industries could trigger a sharp economic slowdown, so the yen should be weakened to support the economy," said an official at another electronics company.\par \par \uc2 Japan has stayed out of the market for four years, the longest stretch in \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data going back to 1991. The last time Japanese authorities sold yen to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was in March 2004, when it was trading at about 108.60 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Japan sold 35 trillion yen ($344 billion) in the 15 months to March 2004 on concerns that excessive yen strength could dampen overseas demand for Japanese exports and hurt the economy.\par \par \uc2 The following is a table of the survey results: DO YOU EXPECT \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  TO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENE\b0 \highlight\uc2  IF YEN RISES BEYOND 95 TO DOLLAR?\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       YES        NO   \par  ------------------------------------------------------------------\par  *ALL                    39%        61%  \par  *MANUFACTURING          37%        63%  \par MATERIALS             36%        64%  \par PROCESSING            38%        62%  \par  *NONMANUFACTURING       41%        59%  \par  WHERE DO YOU EXPECT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO SELL THE YEN?\par               Y92.5-95 Y90-92.5 Y87.5-90 Y85-87.5 Y82.5-85 Y80-82.5 below Y80\par  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par  *ALL                21%      40%      31%       2%      2%      1%       2%\par  *MANUFACTURING      19%      47%      26%       0%      2%      2%       5%\par MATERIALS         20%      47%      27%       0%      0%      0%       7%\par PROCESSING        18%      46%      25%       0%      4%      4%       4%\par  *NONMANUFACTURING   23%      33%      36%       5%      3%      0%       0%\par  DO YOU WANT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO PREVENT YEN APPRECIATION BEYOND Y95 TO\par DOLLAR?\par                       YES        NO     NO PREFERENCE\par  -------------------------------------------------------\par  *ALL                    32%        33%       35%  \par  *MANUFACTURING          38%        33%       29%  \par MATERIALS             33%        40%       28%  \par PROCESSING            41%        29%       30%  \par  *NONMANUFACTURING       24%        33%       42%  \par  WHERE DO YOU EXPECT DOLLAR/YEN AT END-MAY?\par                     AVERAGE    HIGH       LOW   \par  ---------------------------------------------------\par  *ALL                  Y100.58    Y110.00   Y87.00 \par  *MANUFACTURING        Y101.10    Y110.00   Y90.00 \par  *NONMANUFACTURING     Y 99.99    Y110.00   Y87.00 \par \f28 \par