\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,132 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2008\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE:MINUTES HINT BOJ MORE CAUTIOUS ON ECON OUTLOOK IN MAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan board members expressed increasing worry at their March board meeting about the uncertain economy at home and abroad, taking special note of the impact of high energy and raw material prices. (published 0118 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S SWAN: BERNANKE SAYS US GDP WEAK OVER NEXT 6 MOS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke has told Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan that the U.S. economy is slowing and growth in the next six months will not be strong. (published 0057 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM LIMITS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LOANS TO STABILIZE DOMESTIC MKT-OFFICIAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Bank of Vietnam\f28 \uc2 \f2  has requested all banks to tighten their loans in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in an effort to stabilize the domestic market and reduce dollarization, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said Monday. (published 0341 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  NUKAGA: WON'T COMMENT ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister refused to comment Monday on the likelihood of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , saying it's best just to repeat to market participants that Group of Seven officials are concerned about sharp swings in exchange rates. (published 0354 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: NZ FEB RETAIL SALES DOWN, CORE SALES TREND FLAT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Retail sales in New Zealand fell in February, data published Monday showed, offering further evidence of softening consumer spending as the domestic economy sputters. Nonetheless, the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to hold interest rates high for some time as it remains focused on intense inflation pressures, economists say. (published 0103 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN FEB HOUSING FINANCE FALLS 5.9% VS JAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The number of housing-finance approvals in Australia fell a seasonally adjusted 5.9% in February from January, the Australian Bureau of Statistics said Monday. (published 0132 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par *SINGAPORE GOVT TO AWARD S$8B IN INFRASTRUCTURE WORK IN '08 (published 0356 GMT)  \par  \par  \par SWIRE PACIFIC SEEKS TO SELL $300 MILLION 10-YEAR BOND -SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swire Pacific Ltd.\f28 \uc2 \f2  is looking to sell a US$300 million 10-year bond after a recent roadshow, a person familiar with the situation said Monday. (published 0336 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA, CHILE INCLUDE SERVICE SECTORS IN FREE-TRADE AGREEMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China and Chile have agreed to include service sectors in the free-trade agreement between the countries that has been in effect since the end of 2006, a statement on China's central government Web site said Sunday. (published 0121 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MORE THAN $80B IN HOT MONEY FLOWED INTO CHINA IN 1Q - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More than US$80 billion in hot money flowed into China's economy in the first quarter of 2008, the Shanghai Securities News reported Monday, citing an official with a government think tank. (published 0153 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA CONSTRUCTION BANK PLANS CNY40 BLN SUBORDINATED BOND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Construction Bank Corp.\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the country's second-largest lender by assets, said it plans to sell up to CNY40 billion worth of subordinated bonds with a maturity of at least 10 years to boost its capital adequacy ratio. (published 0245 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PAKISTAN GOVT TO SELL CONVERTIBLE BONDS LINKED TO OIL CO OGDC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pakistan's government has mandated three banks to prepare the issuance of convertible bonds linked to shares in state-controlled \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oil and Gas Development Corp.\f28 \uc2 \f2 , or OGDC, the government's senior finance officials said Sunday. (published 2233 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S SWAN SAYS GLOOMY WORLD OUTLOOK WILL HIT BUDGET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan has warned the fallout from the global credit crisis will have a significant impact on the framing of next month's federal budget. (published 2237 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =US DOLLAR RISES AFTER G7 BUT ASIAN STOCK FALLS SUPPORT YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar is trading higher Monday after global financial leaders signaled concern about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility in general, though analysts doubt this is the start of a fresh upward leg for the greenback. (published 0033 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PAKISTAN FIN MIN: WORKING ON ECON, BUT OIL SHOCK HURTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pakistan's government is working to bring the country's economy back onto a more sustainable path, but is concerned about the sky-high price of oil, the country's top finance official said Sunday. (published 2236 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TAIWAN TO LET CHINA COS INVEST IN INFRASTRUCTURE PROJS-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan plans to allow major mainland China companies to invest in the island's infrastructure projects, the Economic Daily News reported Monday, citing Vice President-elect Vincent Siew. (published 0059 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINES BIR EXPECTS APRIL REV TO HIT RECORD HIGH - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bureau of Internal Revenue hopes to collect PHP80.6 billion this April, traditionally the highest earning month for the agency, the Philippine Star newspaper reported Monday, quoting an official of the agency. (published 0219 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KOREA DEVELOPMENT BANK\f28 \uc2 \f2  SELLS CHF300 MILLION IN 5-YEAR BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korea Development Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a statement Monday that it sold CHF300 million in five-year bonds last week at a spread of 117 basis points over Swiss franc Libor. (published 0140 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par POLL:MOST IN UK NOT CONFIDENT IN GOVT'S HANDLING OF ECON-AFP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON --U.K. Prime Minister Gordon Brown's government is losing its much-trumpeted reputation for economic competence, according to a poll published Monday. (published 0109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA MINISTRY: EU BLACKLIST OF AIRLINES "UNFAIR" -AFP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JAKARTA --Indonesia on Monday criticized the European Union's failure to remove any of its airlines from a flight blacklist as "unfair," saying much had been done to improve standards. (published 0318 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WSJ: UK TREASURY CHIEF, BANKERS TO VISIT CHINA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid widespread protests of China's actions in Tibet, U.K. Treasury Chief Alistair Darling and a group of senior bankers will travel to Beijing Monday to strengthen financial links between the two countries, a Treasury official said. (published 2300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =AT A GLANCE: WORLD BK PRES CALLS FOR ACTION ON FOOD CRISIS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THE EVENT: The International Monetary Fund and World Bank are holding their annual meetings in Washington on Saturday and Sunday. Finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations met Friday. Dow Jones Newswires coverage of the events can be found by searching the codes N/RPT and R/DC. (published 2232 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par =PREVIEW:NZ 1Q CPI TO REINFORCE RBNZ RESISTANCE TO EASE EARLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Annual inflation in New Zealand is likely to have accelerated in the first quarter, led by higher food and gasoline prices, reaffirming expectationsthat the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to cut interest rates in the near term. (published 0215 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: DOLLAR'S POST-G7 BOUNCE LOOKS A BRIEF HONEYMOON \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Europeans may have succeeded in getting tougher language on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility into the Group of Seven statement, but the dollar is unlikely to enjoy anything other than a brief honeymoon unless coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  becomes a much greater possibility. (published 2208 GMT) [ 14-04-08 0400GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080414e44e0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
