\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:German Mirow:G7 To Discuss  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 ,Fincl Crisis Response   \b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2008\par 18:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Adds details, background) \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BERLIN (Dow Jones)--The Group of Seven leading industrial nations is likely to agree on joint action to deal with the current financial-market crisis, as well as discuss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates as usual at its meeting Friday, German Deputy Finance Minister Thomas Mirow said Tuesday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 "I am very optimistic that we will agree on a very convincing, substantial response, a joint G7 response to the financial-market developments over the past eight months," Mirow told reporters at a briefing on the forthcoming meeting of G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in Washington D.C. "This will mainly be based on the final report of the Financial Stability Forum."   \par \par \uc2 Market participants are waiting for any sign of joint action to boost the dollar or to counter the U.S. housing-market crisis and its financial fallout. The FSF is due to present its final report on the financial turbulence to G7 finance ministers and central bankers this week.   \par \par \uc2 Mirow also said he opposes the idea of adopting new accounting rules as a response to the crisis and the FSF won't call for this. He also said it should be up to each individual country to decide what measures to adopt to deal with the impact of the financial crisis, such as the U.S. has done for  \uc2 Bear Stearns Cos.\uc2  (BSC), the U.K. for lender  \uc2 Northern Rock\uc2  and Germany for its state-owned banks and  \uc2 IKB Deutsche Industriebank AG\uc2  (IKB.XE).   \par \par \uc2 He also said most of the meeting will be dedicated to the world economic outlook and the financial-market situation.   \par \par \uc2 Asked whether \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and cooperation with Japanese officials on the matter will be discussed at the meeting, Mirow said: "Every G7 ministerial meeting also addresses exchange rates. Washington won't be any exception."   \par \par \uc2 Last month, the euro rose to an all-time high of $1.5905, but it has fallen again, last trading at $1.5703 at 1542 GMT, according to EBS.   \par \par \uc2 When asked about a media report that the German finance ministry is opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the weak dollar and considers an ECB interest rate cut as unlikely, Mirow said the German position is "fully in line with the position of the Eurogroup" of 15 euro-zone finance ministers.   \par \par \uc2 He pointed to recent comments by Luxembourg Prime Minister and Finance Minister Jean-Claude Juncker, who heads the Eurogroup, and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet.   \par \par \uc2 Juncker said Monday that the euro zone must continue to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate developments with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S., stressing that any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  must involve international cooperation.   \par \par \uc2 Commenting on the German growth outlook, Mirow said the International Monetary Fund's predictions have now taken into account comments by Germany that the outlook isn't quite as bad as the fund had previously thought.   \par \par \uc2 The IMF now forecasts 1.4% growth for Germany, Finance Minister Peer Steinbrueck said Saturday, after the fund had previously planned to forecast 1.2% growth in its World Economic Outlook that is due Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 The IMF's prediction is less than the 1.6%-1.7% Steinbrueck forecasts for 2008 German economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 Mirow repeated comments from the weekend that Germany's first-quarter real gross domestic product growth was higher than the 0.3% quarterly growth posted in the fourth quarter. For the full year, Mirow said growth should be near or "just above potential."   \par \par \uc2 Mirow also said he expects majority support for the IMF's quota reform and the executive board financial reform plan that proposes to put 403.3 metric tons of the fund's vast gold holdings up for sale.   \par \par \uc2 -By Andrea Thomas, Dow Jones Newswires; +49-30-2888-410; andrea.thomas@dowjones.com [ 04-08-08 1209ET ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020080408e448000bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
