\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan's Govt Proposes Shirakawa To Fill Top BOJ Seat   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2008\par 12:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's government nominated Deputy Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa Monday to become the chief central banker, a choice likely to get parliamentary approval and end weeks of political furor.   \par \par \uc2 The government also proposed to have Hiroshi Watanabe, formerly a vice finance minister for international affairs, replace Shirakawa as a deputy.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been without a leader since March 20 - a first in the post war era. Over the past month, the opposition-controlled Upper house spurned two of the government's picks to take over from former BOJ Gov. Toshihiko Fukui, saying their ties with the finance ministry would make them biased.   \par \par \uc2 But the latest nominations suggest the embarrassing leadership vacuum may be filled soon.   \par \par \uc2 Many senior officials from the Democratic Party of Japan, which along with other smaller opposition parties control the upper chamber, have publicly spoken in favor of giving Shirakawa the job. When Shirakawa was nominated as a deputy governor last month, the DPJ endorsed him, saying the former BOJ official has enough credentials.   \par \par \uc2 Approval from the upper house is essential. The government can nominate anyone for top BOJ posts, but he or she can't get the job without support from both chambers.   \par \par \uc2 Watanabe's fate, meanwhile, remains uncertain given an apparent division among DPJ officials.   \par \par \uc2 Ichiro Ozawa, DPJ leader, on Sunday signaled his opposition to appointing Watanabe. In a television program, Ozawa reiterated the party's stance against "amakudari," a practice where former bureaucrats from ministries get key positions at government-affiliated or private companies after retiring.   \par \par \uc2 But Secretary General Yukio Hatoyama and former DPJ chief Seiji Maehara have expressed their support of Watanabe's nomination.   \par \par \uc2 Last month, the upper house denied the governor post to Toshiro Muto and Koji Tanami, both former administrative vice finance ministers. The opposition parties argued that the two's ties with the finance ministry may cause them to make monetary policy that aids the government's efforts to reduce its debt, but not for overall economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 Parliament is likely to vote on the new candidates in a few days as Prime Minister Yasuo Fukuda wants to send the official BOJ governor, not an acting governor, to the weekend meeting of finance chiefs from the Group of Seven industrialized nations in Washington, D.C.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Tough Times Ahead \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If approved, Shirakawa, who is now acting governor, would take the helm of the bank at a delicate time. After six years of recovery, Japan's export-driven economy is losing momentum because of the U.S. housing-market crisis, which has caused U.S. demand for Japanese products to weaken and the dollar to fall versus the yen, hurting Japanese exporters.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Public comments made by Shirakawa so far suggest he isn't ready to cut interest rates anytime soon to help the decelerating economy. But some analysts speculate that a sharper slowdown could force his hand.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shirakawa, 58, held key positions in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before retiring in 2006 to take a teaching job at Kyoto University. He has been acting governor since March 19, when Fukui ended his five-year term.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Watanabe, who was vice finance minister for international affairs for three years from the summer of 2004, is an expert in tax and international finance issues.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Watanabe has rarely spoken publicly on how the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should steer monetary policy, but his avoidance of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the past indicates he isn't a big fan of action that could affect markets.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the vice finance minister decides whether to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in consultation with the finance minister. But Watanabe never \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell or buy the yen when he was in the post, thanks partly to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weakness at the time. His lack of action, coupled with his soft-spoken, levelheaded tendencies, gave traders the impression that Watanabe generally prefers to let market forces set the prices of financial products.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 07-04-08 1002GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080407e447000lw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
