\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves hit record high $1.016 trln   \b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2008\par 01:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 7 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's second-largest, hit a record-high $1.016 trillion at the end of March, partly as the euro's rise against the dollar boosted the value of euro-denominated bonds in the reserves.   \par \par \uc2 Investment returns and lower U.S. interest rates that pushed up the value of U.S. bonds held in the stockpile also offset a fall in the value of assets held in gold, resulting in about a 0.8 percent increase in the reserves in March.   \par \par \uc2 The reserves rose for the 10th straight month and set a record high for the ninth month in a row, a finance ministry official said.   \par \par \uc2 The euro <EUR=> traded at $1.5797 at the end of March, compared to $1.5232 at the end of February, the ministry said.   \par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury note <US10YT=RR> fell to 3.413 percent at the end of March from 3.513 percent a month earlier, it added.   \par \par \uc2 Japan does not give \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdowns for its external reserves, but past Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has mostly taken the form of dollar buying, suggests most of Tokyo's foreign cash pile is in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves surged after the country \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2003 and early 2004 to help the economy tackle deflation.   \par \par \uc2 It sold a total of 35 trillion yen ($344 billion) for dollars in the 15 months to March 2004, during which the dollar traded between 105 and 120 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has kept out of the market since then, but income on the reserves has kept them edging up.   \par \par \uc2 The massive reserves have stirred a debate among Japanese lawmakers about whether Japan should follow countries like China, which holds the world's largest foreign reserves, into setting up a sovereign wealth fund to invest some of the cash pile.   \par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which manages the reserves, is against the idea, fearing a wealth fund will be too risky.   \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga told the parliament last month that unrealised losses on Japan's $1 trillion foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves amount to about 18.5 trillion yen when the dollar is around 100 yen.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar traded at 99.37 yen <JPY=> at the end of March, down from 104.34 yen a month earlier amid a fears about a U.S. recession and a deepening credit crisis from U.S. subprime woes. ($1=101.68 Yen) JAPAN OFFICIAL RESERVE ASSETS (in billion dollars)   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                     END-MAR       END-FEB        YEAR AGO\par  TOTAL                1,015.587      1,007.981      908.958\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To view the full tables, go to the Web site of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at   \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mof.go.jp/english/e1c006.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mof.go.jp/english/e1c006.htm  }}}\uc2 \f2  (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/RESERVES (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|NAT|U|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080406e446000nt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
