\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Yen's low value due to slow reform, BOJ policy   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Chikako Mogi and Izumi Nakagawa   \par \uc2 772 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2008\par 04:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 2 (Reuters) - Slow reform, meagre economic growth and low Japanese interest rates are all keeping the yen's real trade-weighted value near a 22-year low, a former top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said.   \par \par \uc2  "The image of Japan pulling out of its 'lost decade' and rebuilding itself has crumbled only a few years into the 21st century, dampening foreign appetite for investment," said Toyoo Gyohten, President of the Institute for International Monetary Affairs.   \par \par \uc2  With consumer prices climbing at a rate of about 1 percent a year, conditions are ripe for the Bank of Japan to raise rates, and its failure to do so "will definitely have a yen-weakening effect," Gyohten told Reuters in an interview on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2  The yen's real effective exchange rate index rose above 100 in March for the first time since September 2006 but stayed near a 22-year low of 90.9 hit last year.   \par \par \uc2  The last time it was so low was in 1985, when the five leading industrialised nations agreed to let the dollar depreciate against the German mark and the yen via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut the bulging U.S. trade deficit in the Plaza Accord.   \par \par \uc2  This suggests that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent climb to a 13-year high against the dollar is more about the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness than about the yen's strength.   \par \par \uc2  Hopes for yen-supportive fiscal reform were dashed when the ruling coalition lost its majority in the upper house of parliament last year, he said. This has come at the same time as a peak in economic cycles and company efforts to maximise profitability after shedding debt and inventory.   \par \par \uc2  The parliamentary deadlock means both BOJ monetary policy and government fiscal policy have reached an impasse, while structural problems are not being dealt with, Gyohten said.   \par \par \uc2  "With the BOJ keeping interest rates at 0.5 percent this long, monetary policy has become irrelevant, as has fiscal policy given the huge public debt," Gyohten said.   \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has been on hold since raising rates by 25 basis points to 0.5 percent in February 2007.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's outstanding government debt stands at about 770 trillion yen ($7.57 billion), equivalent to about 150 percent of the nation's gross domestic product, the highest ratio among major industrialised nations.   \par \par \uc2  Although the economy grew a surprisingly strong 0.9 percent in the last quarter of 2007, economists expect growth to slow in 2008 amid fears that the U.S. economy -- Japan's No.2 trading partner after China -- is heading into recession.   \par \par \uc2  NO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  The global view is that exchange rates are the result of macroeconomic policies and should not be used to affect the economy, said Gyohten, who was director general of the MOF's international bureau at the time of the Plaza Accord and vice finance minister for international affairs when he retired in 1989.   \par \par \uc2  "It's now the global mainstream view -- shared also by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is wrong," he said.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves topped $1 trillion in February, the world's second largest after China, as a result of aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 and early 2004 to stem the yen's rise. The buildup has led some academics and politicians to call for the creation of a special government investment fund to more actively manage the reserves.   \par \par \uc2  Gyohten noted that while Japan must more effectively manage its money, not only the reserves but also the country's massive $14.75 trillion in household financial assets, changing the way it does this will be complex due to legal and technical issues.   \par \par \uc2  As for the recent political standoff that has left the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the hands of an acting governor for the first time in over 80 years, Gyohten criticised the opposition for demanding that politics be kept separate from monetary and fiscal policy.   \par \par \uc2  "One of the key requirements for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor is that the person must be familiar with politics, know how to deal with politicians and be able to convince them of the policy the bank is pursuing," he said.   \par \par \uc2  "It's impossible for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be unrelated to politics. The opposition's claim that politics be separated from monetary and fiscal policies is nonsense."   \par \par \uc2  He added that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor needed to be familiar with financial markets and have access to and be adept at communicating with global counterparts.  (Editing by Hugh Lawson)       \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-GYOHTEN/ (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080405e44200019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
