\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Forex \highlight\uc2  Market Non- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Enters 5th Year   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 13:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government stayed out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in March, extending its period of non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to four years even as the soaring yen threatens the nation's export-driven economy.   \par \par \uc2 The government may have refrained from stepping in due to the U.S. economy possibly entering into a recession, the dollar's broad weakness, and global opposition to attempts by Asian countries to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak to boost exports, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts speculate that Japan may keep a low profile even if the U.S. dollar falls below the 12-and-a-half-year low of Y95.77 set on March 17.   \par \par \uc2 Data released Monday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed that it didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from Feb. 28 through March 27. This means the ministry hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for four years, the longest stretch since such data were released in 1991.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood at Y99.50 around 1144 GMT Monday, down 4% from the start of the month and 11% lower since the beginning of this year.   \par \par \uc2 A weaker dollar makes Japanese exports more expensive in the U.S. and other dollar-linked markets. Prime Minister Yasuo Fukuda, Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga and Economy Minister Hiroko Ota have all expressed concern over the rising yen over the past few weeks, as recent data were already signaling a weakening economy.   \par \par \uc2 "It will be tough for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the U.S. and Europe agree to the action," said Koji Fukaya, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Deutsche Securities.   \par \par \uc2 But "the U.S. will probably keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue on the back burner" for a while as it focuses on soothing jittery domestic credit markets and since a weaker dollar benefits the U.S. economy, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Any moves by Japan to shore up the dollar to help its exporters could anger U.S. manufacturers and possibly Washington, given that the U.S. economy faces a greater risk of recession than Japan, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar's weakness is also too broad for Tokyo to counter on its own, analysts say. "Even if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone, there may be little impact," said Yuji Kameoka, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at  \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research.\uc2  "The markets are too big, and moreover, the root of dollar weakness is in the U.S., which Japan can do little about."   \par \par \uc2 Selling the yen could also hurt Japan's credibility, given that it, along with the U.S. and Europe, has been pushing China to let the yuan rise faster, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 Economic Pickup   \par \par \uc2 The finance ministry's hands-off approach over the last four years contrasts with its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over a 15-month period that ended March 16, 2004.   \par \par \uc2 The ministry, which determines the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, sold an unprecedented Y35 trillion in total, mostly for dollars, to weaken the yen and in turn support domestic exporters.   \par \par \uc2 The inaction since could be attributed partly to a strengthening domestic economy.   \par \par \uc2 "Back then, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign was part of a broad policy to pull the economy out of deflation," which threatened to snuff out the country's then-embryonic economic recovery, said Fukaya of Deutsche Securities.   \par \par \uc2 But that recovery, beginning early 2002, has since gained more muscle, Fukaya said. Banks cut bad loans while companies reduced their workforces and excess capacity, so "in terms of the economy, the government didn't have legitimate reasons for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2 Despite exports, industrial output and corporate profits falling on the back of a slowing U.S. economy, the Japanese economy remains in better shape than four years ago, and traders say they see little chance of near-term \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 31-03-08 1147GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080331e43v000t1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
