\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,191 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE: BOJ SUDA: STILL NATURAL BOJ WILL AIM FOR RATE HIKE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan board member Miyako Suda Thursday expressed alarm about the outlook for the country's economy, but indicated her opposition to an early rate cut amid the current "very easy" monetary conditions. (published 0453 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/27)UPDATE:PAULSON FAVORS BOOSTING SCRUTINY OF WALL STREET    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson's suggestion Wednesday that the U.S. Federal Reserve play a greater role in monitoring investment banks places him in a growing chorus arguing that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should have more oversight of Wall Street. (published 0019 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: NISHIMURA: JAPAN ECON OUTLOOK UNCERTAINTY HIGH NOW    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Deputy Gov. Kiyohiko Nishimura said Thursday that uncertainty over the nation's economic outlook is "especially high" right now. (published 0239 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UN: EXPECT ASIA PACIFIC DEVELOPING ECONS TO GROW 7.7% IN 2008    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The developing economies in the Asia Pacific region are likely to grow 7.7% in 2008, according to a report released Thursday by the United Nations Economic and Social Commission for Asia and the Pacific, or UNESCAP. (published 0609 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: JAPAN FRETS OVER YEN, BUT NO THREAT OF ACTION    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister voiced discontent Thursday with the yen's surge last week, but he highlighted some advantages of a strong domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and didn't signal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will soon \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken it. (published 0627 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  NUKAGA: FROM L/T VIEW STRONG YEN MAY BE PLUS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga said Thursday that a strong yen may benefit Japan's economy in the long run. (published 0440 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK INDONESIA DEPUTY GOV: NO NEED TO HIKE RATE FOR NOW    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia Senior Deputy Governor Miranda Goeltom said Thursday that there's no need for rate hikes to stem inflation for now. (published 0421 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE:AUSTRALIA COPING WELL DESPITE WORLD MKT STORM-RBA    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The global financial system is under the most strain it has had to face since the early 1990s, but Australia is coping well by comparison with other countries. (published 0538 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: AUSTRALIA PM: FINCL REGULATION SHOULD BE MORE GLOBAL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd said Thursday that the regulation of financial systems should be more global and the Reserve Bank of Australia and Treasury officials are already in talks with their offshore counterparts about managing the ongoing global credit crisis. (published 2303 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UN REPORT: EXPECT INDIA ECON TO GROW 8.5%-9.5% MEDIUM TERM    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's economy is expected to grow between 8.5% and 9.5% over the medium term, according to a report released Thursday by United Nations Economic and Social Commission for Asia and the Pacific, or UNESCAP. (published 0547 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: NZ FIN MIN: NZ ECON WON'T EXPERIENCE HARD LANDING    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand Finance Minister Michael Cullen said Thursday that the domestic economy is unlikely to experience a hard landing, despite expectations that economic growth will slow in the period ahead. (published 0259 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE:NZ 4Q CURR ACCT DEFICIT SHRINKS, DAIRY-LIFT HELPS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's current account deficit shrank in the fourth quarter, driven by a dairy-led boost to exports, confirming market expectations for the deficit to become smaller over the next year. (published 0006 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN JOB VACANCIES -2.5% FEB QTR VS NOV QTR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian job vacancies fell 2.5% in seasonally adjusted terms during the three-month period ended February 2008 from the previous three-month period, the Australian Bureau of Statistics reported Thursday. (published 0032 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: FEW SIGNS AUSTRALIA HIGH HOUSING COSTS IMPROVING-RBA    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 High housing costs are set to persist in the near term with both supply and demand side factors feeding the problem, Tony Richards, head of economic analysis at the Reserve Bank of Australia, said Thursday. (published 0401 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM JAN-MAR FOREIGN DIRECT INVEST PLEDGES +31% AT $5.436B    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Newly pledged foreign direct investment to Vietnam is estimated to have risen 31% on year to $5.436 billion in the first quarter this year, the Ministry of Planning and Investment said in a statement Thursday. (published 0319 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MALAYSIA NEW TRADE MIN: NO RUSH TO FORGE US TRADE DEAL - AFP    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's new trade minister said Thursday there was no rush to sign a free-trade deal which the United States hopes could be struck before President George W. Bush leaves office this year. (published 0646 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BAHRAIN ISLAMIC BK IN TALKS WITH BANKS TO SELL $663M SUKUK    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bahrain Islamic Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  is currently in talks with regional and international banks to sell 250 million Bahraini dinars of sukuk, a banker said. (published 0547 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UAE'S FIRST GULF BANK TO SELL $1 BLN CONV BOND THU - SOURCE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 First Gulf Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  may sell as early as March 27 at least $1 billion worth of convertible bonds to a group of private investors and investment funds, a banker familiar with the deal said. (published 0523 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NATL AUSTRALIA BK RAISES A$725M VIA TAP OF 2011 NOTES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 National Australia Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  has raised A$725 million via a tap of its existing June 2011 notes, lead manager nabCapital said Thursday. (published 0014 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SINGAPORE MAS SELLS 5-YEAR GOVT BOND AT 1.65% CUTOFF YIELD    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore Thursday sold S$2.5 billion of five-year government bonds at a cutoff yield of 1.65%, meeting market expectations. (published 0514 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA PENSION FUND TO STOP BUYING US TREASURYS - FT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's  \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Pension Service\f28 \uc2 \f2  will stop buying U.S. Treasury securities because their yields are too low, the Financial Times reported on its Web site Wednesday. (published 2008 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA EXPECTS '08 FUEL SUBSIDIES +40% ON YR TO IDR126.82T    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesian government expects fuel subsidies to increase nearly 40% in 2008 to IDR126.82 trillion due to rising global crude oil prices, a parliament member said late Wednesday. (published 0131 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: KOREA MAR CPI LIKELY JUMPED ON WEAKER WON, HIGH OIL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's inflation rate likely accelerated in March, largely propelled by the recent plunge in the Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and high global oil and raw material costs, economists said, suggesting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may keep its benchmark rate unchanged in the near term despite the government's pro-growth policy. (published 0604 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: FED OFFICIALS DEFEND NEW LENDING MECHANISMS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two Federal Reserve officials emerged back onto the stage Wednesday, determined to impress the importance of providing sufficient cash to all banks at this time of crisis. (published 2155 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: THE ICELANDIC SHIVER FOR ASIA    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Things are looking a bit chilly in Reykjavik these days, and the ill wind might yet blow towards emerging markets in Asia. (published 2354 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =INTERVIEW: AUSTRALIAN GOVT PLANS MINOR CHANGES TO CORP LAW    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian government's corporate law minister, Nick Sherry, said Thursday that a government review into potential loopholes in the Corporations Act that may have exacerbated recent financial market volatility is "well advanced" and has identified changes that are needed to the act. (published 0638 GMT) [ 27-03-08 0700GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080327e43r0008j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
