\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD: Foreign CBs Snap Up Tsys Even As Dlr Tumbles\b0\par\par\uc2 1,232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 18:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Min Zeng \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Conventional wisdom says a falling dollar risks speeding up diversification away from U.S. assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Conventional wisdom, it seems, is wrong - at least when it comes to foreign central banks. Their love affair with Treasury bonds has continued even as the dollar has slumped across the board and yields have plunged to multi-year lows. They've moved away from risky assets, such as corporate debt and stocks, putting their money instead back into Treasurys and agency debt.\par \par \uc2 Buying in Treasurys has steadily increased since the subprime mortgage turmoil started to wreak havoc on global credit markets last summer, data from the Federal Reserve showed.\par \par \uc2 Rather than drive these safety-conscious investors away, the plunging dollar has turned high-quality U.S. assets into big bargains. Unlike hedge funds and other short term players, central banks act more like long-term investors who don't frequently and actively manage their holdings.\par \par \uc2 Demand for Treasurys has pushed down yields on government bonds across the board. The two-year note was yielding 1.61% Wednesday and the 10-year note 3.41%. They have fallen from highs of 5.13% and 5.32%, respectively, last June. The bond equivalent yield on three-month T-bills plunged the weakest level since the 1950s this week.\par \par \uc2 "There is so much uncertainty on the credit side and on the economy. The logical place to go is into Treasurys until people feel the smoke has cleared," said William O'Donnell, head of U.S. government bond strategy at \uc2 UBS Securities LLC\uc2  in Stamford, Conn.\par \par \uc2 "Even though Treasurys look expensive to us, for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holders, they are much cheaper, at least in those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which have strengthened so much to the dollar," he added.\par \par \uc2 Marketable Treasurys held in foreign official and international accounts at the Federal Reserve rose to $1.28 trillion for the week ended March 12, up from $1.2 trillion over the same period in September and $1.13 trillion two years ago, according to data from the Fed compiled by Louis Crandall, chief economist at Wrightson ICAP LLC, a Jersey City, N.J.-based research firm that focuses on government debt.\par \par \uc2 Institutional investors from Japan including the Bank of Japan have snapped up Treasurys over the past three months - a time that usually sees them repatriate money home for the fiscal year that closes on March 31, according to bond traders who observed the flows.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Japan, China Add To Stash \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan, the largest holder of U.S. Treasury securities, boosted its holdings to $586.9 billion in January from $581.2 billion a month earlier, the latest data from U.S. Treasury Department showed Monday. China, the second-largest holder of U.S. Treasurys, also lifted holdings to $492.6 billion from $477.6 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Buying Treasurys is also a way to maintain stable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates for countries that peg their local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the U.S. dollar. The more the dollar plunges, the bigger the need for them to sell local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to maintain the pegs, and the larger the dollar amount they accumulate to recycle back into U.S. assets like Treasurys, strategists say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For example, Saudi Arabia's monetary agency, which holds mostly U.S. assets, has added an average of $15 billion a month for the last four months, said Rachel Ziemba, an analyst in New York at RGE Monitor.com, an online economic research company that closely monitors foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  activities in the Treasurys market. Saudi Arabia's riyal is pegged to the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The United Arab Emirates' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  added $20 billion in U.S. government securities in the first nine months of last year, up from about $6 billion a year before, she added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These central banks are under a lot of pressure, as keeping their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weak to maintain the dollar peg has fueled inflation pressures and raised speculation they may need to remove the links with the greenback.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tumbling dollar has also raised speculation of official \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the euro zone as their exports feel the pinch. A relatively stronger local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  damps the competitiveness of their goods and services in global markets and undermines broad economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pervasive view in the markets right now is that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the euro zone will refrain from outright \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. Instead, they may express concern over the falling dollar in the statement by the Group of Seven leading nations, which meet next month in Washington.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G7 consists of the U.S., the U.K., Canada, Japan, Germany, France and Italy. The last time major central banks \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  jointly in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets was September 2000, when they propped up a falling euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par             Disguised \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Jim O'Neill, head of global economic research at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc.\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London, said some central banks may have boosted holdings of Treasurys and other U.S. assets as a way of "disguised \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " to prevent the falling dollar move from being disorderly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Instead of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  directly in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, foreign central banks literally increased the degree of U.S. assets" they purchase, he said. "They just decided to increase (their) purchases of Treasurys and reduce the speed of diversification."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stephen Jen, head of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London, said such an idea is "very sensible" because few of the large reserve holders want to see the dollar collapse.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the exception of Russia, most other large central banks still have the bulk of their reserves in dollars, Jen noted. "This makes it very much in their interest not to undermine the dollar. Slower diversification makes sense. But it is difficult to prove."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's downturn has moved into high gear over the past few weeks as aggressive interest rate cuts from the Federal Reserve, the deepening credit-market crunch and fear of recession have led to fire-sales of the greenback. The dollar has set record lows against the euro and the Swiss franc and more than 13-year lows against the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, buying in Treasurys may not be sustainable if aggressive rate cuts from the Fed and fiscal stimulus from the government help revive credit markets and get growth get back to its feet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Once market sentiment turns, investors should be prepared for heavy selling of Treasurys, with yields set to back up very quickly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The bid for Treasurys will continue while the fear trade holds sway," said Theodore Ake, head of U.S. Treasury trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  USA Inc. in New York. "However, when the panic has hit bottom and investors start to look at real yields, everyone will be dumping Treasurys to go elsewhere."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Min Zeng, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2096; min.zeng@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Min Zeng writes about Treasurys markets for Dow Jones Newswires)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments [ 03-19-08 1341ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4083   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020080319e43j0006c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
