\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls vs euro on Fed meeting; up vs yen   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vivianne Rodrigues   \par \uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 12:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds comments)   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Reuters) - The dollar traded near record lows against the euro Tuesday amid expectations for an aggressive Federal Reserve interest rate cut, while it rebounded versus the yen as stocks rallied and investors' appetite for risk revived.   \par \par \uc2 Markets are pricing in a cut of at least 100 basis points to the Fed's benchmark lending rate from the current 3 percent as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to revive the struggling economy. The Federal Reserve's policy-setting panel, which meets on Tuesday, is expected to issue a decision around 2:15 p.m. (1815 GMT).   \par \par \uc2 Expectations of rate cuts deepened after  \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 's deal to purchase stricken rival  \uc2 Bear Stearns\uc2  for a rock-bottom $2 a share and the Fed's emergency step of cutting its discount rate by 25 basis points on Sunday.   \par \par \uc2 The greenback, which sold off on Monday, remained under pressure on Tuesday, although it advanced against the yen as stronger-than-expected results from  \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  and  \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  eased some investors' concerns about the health of the U.S. financial sector.   \par \par \uc2 Demand for the Japanese yen tends to increase as investors' aversion to risk grows. As U.S. stocks rallied, with investors willing to take on more risk, the dollar recouped some of its Monday's sharp losses versus the low-yielding yen.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar on Monday had tumbled to record lows versus the euro, Swiss franc and a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and had fallen further below the 100 yen mark.   \par \par \uc2 "There were still fears lingering about possible liquidity problems in banks such as  \uc2 Lehman Brothers.\uc2  But given the bank released better-than-expected results, in combination with another solid report by Goldman, the dollar bounced back against the yen," said Mark Meadows, a market strategist at Tempus Consulting in Washington.   \par \par \uc2 "But the rate decision later today is still weighing on the greenback against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2 Meadows said he expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut the benchmark rate by 75 basis points.   \par \par \uc2 The euro was up 0.3 percent at $1.5768, moving toward Monday's record peak of $1.5904. The dollar was down 0.2 percent against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 71.335.   \par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar, meanwhile, rose to session highs versus the greenback as above-forecast February core inflation data was seen limiting the scope for future rate cuts.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up 0.9 percent on the day versus the low-yielding yen but, at 98.32 yen, stayed under the 100 mark breached last week. It also remained below parity at 0.9911 Swiss francs.   \par \par \uc2 "Markets have stabilized but that's not changing the fundamental situation in the U.S.," said Geoffrey Yu, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at  \uc2 UBS\uc2  in Zurich.   \par \par \uc2 "There is speculation the Fed could cut rates by 100 basis points. That's going to put the U.S. as the second lowest-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the G10, below even the Swiss franc. We might be entering the stage when people will be looking at the dollar as a funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  EYED   \par \par \uc2 The global credit squeeze continued to be reflected in short-term money markets, with overnight U.S. dollar deposit rates rising as high as 3.85 percent on Tuesday, having moved above 4 percent on Monday .   \par \par \uc2 The dollar's sharp sell-off on Monday led traders to fret about the possibility of joint dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by U.S., Japanese and European authorities.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Naoyuki Shinohara, said he was told by Prime Minister Yasuo Fukuda to keep in close contact with other Group of Seven members on developments in global financial markets.   \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have recently expressed concern about "excessive" moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but analysts say the ECB may be willing to accept the strong euro to help rein in inflation.   \par \par \uc2 The ECB's main task is to keep prices stable and avoid second-round inflation effects, ECB board member Lorenzo Bini Smaghi told a Brazilian newspaper, adding that exports have held up well in recent years despite the rise in the euro. (Additional reporting by Tony Vorobyova; Editing by Leslie Adler)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 8)|LANGEN|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080321e43i000hm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
