\par\par \b \uc2 Goldman's O'Neill Sees Disguised  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  In Tsys Demand   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Min Zeng   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 17:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Foreign central banks may have bought Treasurys and other U.S. assets as a way to prevent the dollar's plunge from becoming disorderly, said Jim O'Neill, head of global economic research at  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc.\f28 \uc2 \f2  (GS) in London.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A slumping dollar may actually be a blessing in disguise for Treasurys as foreign central banks can snap up the top-quality papers at a big bargain, he said. Foreign central banks are long-term investors, aiming for stability, he added.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Foreign official institutions such as central banks bought a net $36.1 billion of Treasurys in January, sharply up from net purchases of just $11 billion the previous month, according to a U.S. Treasury Department report released Monday. Japan and China, the two largest foreign owners of Treasurys, increased their buying over the same period, the data showed.   \par \par \uc2 "There may have been some kind of disguised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said O'Neill in a phone interview from his London office Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 "Instead of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, foreign central banks literally increase the degree of U.S. assets" they purchased, he said. "They just decided to increase purchases of Treasurys and reduce the speed of diversification."   \par \par \uc2 The dollar's downturn has moved into high gear over the past few weeks as aggressive interest rate cuts from the Federal Reserve, a deepening credit-market crunch and fear of recession have led to a fire-sale in the greenback.   \par \par \uc2 Over the past month, the yen and euro have surged 10% and 7.7% against the dollar. The dollar has set record lows against the euro and the Swiss franc and a more than 13 year low against the yen.   \par \par \uc2 The tumbling dollar has raised speculation of official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as exports from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and euro zone are feeling the pinch - a relatively stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  damps the competitiveness of their goods and services in global markets and undermines broad economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 "The dilemma is that the dollar weakness can build on itself. So one has to be really careful," said O'Neill. "I think the recent move in the dollar has been close to be disorderly - close. Dollar-yen went from 102 to 96 between Thursday night and Monday morning. That is big."   \par \par \uc2 The pervasive view in the markets right now is that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro zone will refrain from actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Instead, they may express concern over the falling dollar in the statement that regularly follows the Group of Seven meeting next month in Washington.   \par \par \uc2 O'Neill noted that many factors including continued rate cuts from the Fed run counter to the possibility of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Besides, the yen is still relatively cheap versus the euro, even though the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been strengthening against the dollar, he said.   \par \par \uc2 "It will have to involve the euro as well. Otherwise you couldn't get coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said O'Neill. "Most people have a fair value for euro-yen at around 125, but here we are at 155."   \par \par \uc2 O'Neill said the deadlock in the nomination of the new Bank of Japan governor won't be a factor in whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will go for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or not. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is determined by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not the BOJ, he noted.   \par \par \uc2 -By Min Zeng, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; min.zeng@dowjones.com [ 03-18-08 1246ET ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020080318e43i00071\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
