\par\par \b \uc2 Sakakibara, Mr Yen, says  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2  unlikely   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dan Sloan   \par \uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 08:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 17 (Reuters) - Japanese officials are unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to cap their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the dollar slides to levels such as 70 yen, because the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fairly weak on a broad basis, said Eisuke Sakakibara, a former top official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara, known as "Mr Yen" for for spearheading the MOF's yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  while vice finance minister in the 1990s, told Reuters that the dollar may fall to 85 yen and the MOF would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because such levels only translate to dollar levels of about 110 yen a decade ago.   \par \par \uc2 "It's up to the authorities in Tokyo and Washington D.C., so I cannot precisely predict what they will do. But I would not expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 85," Sakakibara said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar plunged 3 percent to a 13-year low of 95.77 yen on Monday, before rebounding a little, as investors were rattled by fears there could be more casualties in the spreading U.S. financial crisis.   \par \par \uc2 Credit turmoil prompted  \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  to take over stricken investment bank  \uc2 Bear Stearns\par \par \uc2 The dollar's tumble to record lows against the euro and Swiss franc has accelerated in the past few weeks, stirring speculation that the United States, Europe and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may conduct joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since 2000 to stem the slide.   \par \par \uc2 But Sakakibara, who is now a professor at Waseda University, said such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely just yet.   \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. will not approve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Japanese authorities will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of the real rate of exchange. Of course, the move is a little too fast."   \par \par \uc2 Even as the yen has powered higher against the dollar, it remains somewhat weak against other higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Australian dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell through 100 yen for the first time since 1995 last Thursday. But compared to late 1995, the yen's real trade-weighted value is about 25 percent less as of February.   \par \par \uc2 "Even 90 or 85 is 110 ten years ago. In real terms the yen has depreciated very substantially and I have called that the 'cheap yen bubble' and what's happening is the bursting of the cheap yen bubble," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "If the rate will become something like 70 or 60, then that might be the time to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga stepped up his verbal warnings on Monday, saying that he will watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves in cooperation with U.S. and European authorities. (Writing by Eric Burroughs, editing by Edwina Gibbs)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /SAKAKIBARA (INTERVIEW, TV)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080320e43h000dp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
