\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE:Asia Stks Dn; Bear News, Fed Move Fail To Support   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 07:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian share markets and the U.S. dollar have come off their session lows but remain under pressure Monday as another round of liquidity measures by the Federal Reserve and a planned buyout of  \uc2 Bear Stearns Cos.\uc2  (BSC) fail to convince investors that the worst is over.   \par \par \uc2 A flight to quality has U.S. Treasurys and the region's government bonds surging, and the dropping dollar has sent crude oil and gold prices to record highs. Spreadbettors and the futures markets are pricing in sharp declines in U.S. and European stocks.   \par \par \uc2 A fall in Asian shares was already underway early Monday before news emerged that  \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co.\uc2  (JPM) had struck a deal to buy  \uc2 Bear Stearns\uc2 , and that the Fed had cut its discount rate by a quarter point to 3.25%.   \par \par \uc2 But far from soothing nerves, the news only served to raise worries about what's to come from some of Bear's rivals in the week ahead, and from the Fed's interest rate policy meeting on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 "The Fed is busy announcing emergency measures because the U.S. financial system probably is under more strain now than for at least several decades," said Rory Roberston, interest rate strategist at  \uc2 Macquarie Bank.\par \par \uc2 The $2 per share price tag for  \uc2 Bear Stearns\uc2  has alarmed many, underscoring the trouble in the U.S. banking system. The stock was nearly $60 a share before news that it was facing a liquidity crisis was first disclosed.   \par \par \uc2 "The price at which it has gone is a shocker, and the grapevine is that there could be more investment banks with problems," said a hedge fund manager in India who has assets of over $2 billion invested in Asia.   \par \par \uc2 Some questions may be answered this week with  \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc.\uc2  (GS) and Lehman Brother's Holdings Inc. (LEH) due to report fiscal first-quarter earnings on Tuesday, with  \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  (MS) to follow Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 But few are expecting good news. The U.K.'s Telegraph newspaper reported on its Web site that  \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  will this week announce asset writedowns worth about $3 billion.   \par \par \uc2 And as analysts at ING wrote to clients, shares of  \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  significantly underperformed the broad market on Friday - falling nearly 15% - and offered a clue as to which bank investors are worried about.   \par \par \uc2 Lehman may not get the support from the Fed that  \uc2 Bear Stearns\uc2  did, they added.   \par \par \uc2 "We think the Fed was moved to provide lender of last resort facilities because it judged Bear's large prime brokerage business made it 'too big to fail' in the wholesale payments, clearing and settlement system. On the face of it, Lehman is not too big to fail."   \par \par \uc2 Investors are also left to speculate what the Fed will do during its Tuesday meeting.   \par \par \uc2 Expectations are mounting for a cut in the Fed funds rate of a full percentage point with  \uc2 UBS\uc2  noting the overnight indexed swap market is now pricing in 90.5 basis points in cuts, up from 71.5 basis points on Friday. June Eurodollar futures are being quoted at an implied yield of 1.865% compared to Friday's close of 2.085% in the U.S.   \par \par \uc2 "A week ago, a 100 basis point cut may have seemed unthinkable to some. Now a cut from 3% to 2% is entirely possible, maybe even close to 'central case'," Macquarie's Robertson said.   \par \par \uc2 As well as the discount rate cut, the Fed announced it was extending primary credit loans (for primary dealers) to 90 days from 30 days. Still, previous liquidity actions haven't done much more than provide a temporary reprieve from the credit crunch.   \par \par \uc2 "The liquidity problems are not due to interest rates being too high but rather banks not being prepared to lend to each other because of counterparty risk," said Benjamin Pedley, executive director at LGT Bank in Liechtenstein.   \par \par \uc2 Stocks Down, But Off Of Session Lows   \par \par \uc2 The retreat in Asian stocks has U.S. futures in the red and weakening. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures are down 1.8% or 217 points in electronic trade after initially gaining.   \par \par \uc2 Spreadbettors are calling for sizable declines in European markets, with CMC Markets calling a 100 point drop in London's FTSE 100, a 140 point drop in Frankfurt's DAX and a 122 point drop in the CAC-40 in Paris.   \par \par \uc2 Among Asian markets, Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index is down 4.0% to 21,346.32, making it one of the worst-performing benchmarks in the region. Still, the index is well off of its low of the session of 21,0041.26.   \par \par \uc2 Also faring poorly are stocks in India with the Sensex off 4.9% at 14995.1.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore's Straits Times Index meanwhile is down 2% at 2780.92, but above its low of 2745.96, and the Jakarta Composite in Indonesia is down 3.9%.   \par \par \uc2 Among markets that have already closed, the Nikkei 225 dropped 3.7% to 11,787.50, ending at its lowest level since August 2005 - but above its session low of 11,691. Banking stocks were particularly weak with Acom (8572.TO) down 4.6% and MUFG (8306.TO) down 4.8%.   \par \par \uc2 Also faring poorly in Japan were exporters.  \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) dropped 5.7%, and Toyota (7203.TO) fell 5.1%.   \par \par \uc2 The Kospi Composite in Korea fell 1.6% to 1,574.44, and Australia's S&P/ASX 200 dropped 2.3% to 5087.   \par \par \uc2 Dollar Remains Under Pressure   \par \par \uc2 Reflecting rising risk aversion, the Japanese yen surged against a slew of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the safe-haven Swiss franc touched a record high against the U.S. dollar. The euro also hit a fresh record against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The euro was recently at US$1.5845, down a tad from its record high of US$1.5905 reached earlier. The dollar was at CHF0.9746, above its record low of CHF0.9572 earlier Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was recently down around Y97.27 - after hitting Y95.76 - with the euro trading around Y153.90.   \par \par \uc2 Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options have soared in Tokyo with dollar/yen volatility going over 24%, the highest level in about ten years.   \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are weaker amid risk aversion with the Australian and New Zealand dollars declining against both the U.S. dollar and the yen. The Australian dollar is down to US$0.9256, and the New Zealand dollar was recently at US$0.8071.   \par \par \uc2 The Korean won and Singapore dollar are notable decliners against the U.S. dollar while the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been spotted selling the U.S. dollar to support the rupiah and Korean authorities have warned about the pace of the won's slide.   \par \par \uc2 The won, in fact, suffered its biggest one day decline against the dollar since August 1998. The dollar was recently at KRW1024.90.   \par \par \uc2 A broad U.S. dollar recovery is some months off as the U.S. needs to be at least halfway through a recession before investors start buying assets there, said Lehman Brothers Australia chief economist Stephen Roberts.   \par \par \uc2 "There's not a lot of reason yet to be looking at the U.S. dollar and saying it's bargain basement. We're not at that point yet," he said.   \par \par \uc2 There will be some focus on Japanese authorities as the yen continues to weaken against the yen under the Y100 mark, with Y90 viewed by some as a potential "line in the sand".   \par \par \uc2 Still,  \uc2 Bank of New Zealand\uc2  strategist Danica Hampton doubts the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  anytime soon, with a stronger yen helping to offset some of the costs of higher crude oil prices.   \par \par \uc2 "I think what will be the trigger is if we did see the yen strengthen markedly against the Chinese yuan, and I think that's the one to watch."   \par \par \uc2 Flight-To-Quality Lifts Treasurys, Government Bonds   \par \par \uc2 Government bonds are gaining on flight-to-quality bids with the yield on 10-year U.S. Treasurys briefly falling to 3.35%, a two-month low, and the 2-year yield to 1.24%, the lowest level since June 2003. More recently the 2-year yield was at 1.28% and the 10-year at 3.34%.   \par \par \uc2 Lead 10-year Japanese Government Bond futures touched 141.13 - their highest level since July 2005 - from a 140.27 close on Friday. They were more recently at 141.28.   \par \par \uc2 Credit default swaps have widened in Australia and Japan. In Japan, Promise (8574.TO) 5-year protection has been done 65 basis points wider at 400 basis points. The broad iTraxx Japan Series 8 has traded as wide as 245 basis points from the 213-basis point close on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Spot gold has benefited from U.S. dollar weakness, trading at a record of $1,032.35 a troy ounce, up $29.85 from the New York close.   \par \par \uc2 Front-month Nymex crude is 89 cents higher on Globex at $111.10 a barrel, after touching a record $111.42, helped too by the U.S. dollar cue.   \par \par \uc2 "The sooner the Fed is done cutting interest rates, the better. The sooner it is done, almost regardless of the actual figure it cuts to, the more likely oil markets are to return to their own supply and demand factors," said Peter Beutel at trading advisory firm Cameron Hanover.   \par \par \uc2 "The longer that the Fed takes to cut rates, the more dreadful inflation is likely to become."   \par \par \uc2 -By Rosalind Mathieson and Mohammed Hadi; Dow Jones Newswires; rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com; +65-6415-4141   \par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 17-03-08 0643GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080317e43h000a4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
