\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan's MOF Getting More Worried About Surging Yen   \b0\par\par\uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 05:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from analysts, more background.)   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister voiced deeper concern about the soaring yen Monday, calling its sudden surge against the dollar "excessive" - the strongest warning yet from Tokyo, which is concerned a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could harm the economy.   \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga spoke after the dollar plunged Y3.5 to Y95.77 Monday, its lowest since 1995, due to fears about the outlook for the U.S. financial system. The collapse - and the swift U.S. Federal Reserve-sponsored buyout - of  \uc2 Bear Stearns Cos.\uc2  underlined the scope of the U.S. banking credit crisis.   \par \par \uc2 The yen also soared against a slew of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , setting fresh recent highs against the euro, Sterling, the Australian and New Zealand dollars, putting more pressure on Japan's export-dependent economy.   \par \par \uc2 "I feel that recent movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are excessive, and I'm concerned about it," Nukaga told reporters.   \par \par \uc2 But the finance minister, who has the final say on whether Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell or buy the yen against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that for now "we're not thinking about any specific steps."   \par \par \uc2 The comments show the government has raised its alert level on exchange rates but suggest that the ministry isn't about to sell the yen to curb its gains for the first time in four years.   \par \par \uc2 A strong yen raises the chance of a Japanese recession because it makes the country's exports more expensive at a time when exports are all that is propelling the nation's economy forward, economists say.   \par \par \uc2 Until Monday, Nukaga had only repeated that excessive exchange rate moves aren't welcome and that he is watchful of the yen's rise. He changed his language Monday after a brief meeting at his office with Vice Finance Minister For International Affairs Naoyuki Shinohara, indicating that Nukaga's remarks were carefully crafted to let market participants know how alarmed the government is.   \par \par \uc2 "The Japanese government certainly appears more worried" about the surging yen, said Osamu Takashima, chief analyst of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the  \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ.   \par \par \uc2 The euro Monday revisited a three-month low of Y152.10, while the British pound hit the lowest since Sept. 30, 2005 of Y192.61. The Australian dollar dropped to Y88.21, a level not seen since Aug. 17, 2007. The New Zealand dollar also weakened to Y77.04, the lowest since Sept. 10, 2007.   \par \par \uc2 The resurgent yen is a headache for Prime Minister Yasuo Fukuda's Cabinet, whose public approval ratings continue to drop in part because of Fukuda's handling of economic policies and his failure so far to install new Bank of Japan governor quickly.   \par \par \uc2 "In addition to the dollar's weakness (versus the yen), crude oil prices are spiraling upward, while the slumping stock markets are weighing on Japan's corporate sector. This all means that the probability of recession is very high," said Toshihiro Nagahama, a senior economist at Dai-Ichi Life Research Institute.   \par \par \uc2 Nagahama estimated that if the dollar averages Y95.00 and a barrel of crude oil $120.00 in the Japanese fiscal year starting April 1, that could cut corporate earnings before tax payments by 8.7% for that year. Japan's growth rate for fiscal 2008 could be also slashed by 0.7 percentage point, he said.   \par \par \uc2 The Fukuda Cabinet's goal to make the economy expand 2.0% in that fiscal year "is totally impossible," Nagahama said.   \par \par \uc2 Despite the risks that a stronger yen poses to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy, analysts speculate that yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't an imminent threat.   \par \par \uc2 "Though the dollar has fallen sharply versus the yen, exchange rates on the whole suggest the yen ... even could be called still weak," Takashima said, referring to the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's levels against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "I personally do not think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in a situation where it must \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets at all costs. Unless (the Group of Seven industrialized nations) agree on the need of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to underpin the dollar, I don't expect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will act."   \par \par \uc2 Japan may also be reluctant to see its huge \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves increase even more through yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or just can't act after months of pressing China to let the yuan rise faster, other analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 Moreover, "what's happening is the dollar's broad weakness ... that Japan can't fight on its own," Nagahama said. "Interest rate cuts (by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) would be a better policy step than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 17-03-08 0458GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080317e43h0006u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
