\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 12:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =RISING YEN WORRIES JAPAN BUT NO HINTS OF ACTION YET    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's top economy officials voiced renewed concerns Friday about the dollar's recent rapid fall against the yen, but they provided few hints on whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help the embattled U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0142 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: EURO-ZONE FEB CPI REVISED TO +3.3%, ALL-TIME HIGH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The annual rate of euro zone consumer price inflation was revised up to a fresh all-time high of 3.3% Friday, reducing chances of a near-term rate cut from the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 1000 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE'S GIEVE: CPI EXPECTATIONS ARE 'ABSOLUTELY KEY FOR US'    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of England Deputy Governor John Gieve said Friday that keeping inflation expectations anchored is "absolutely key" to the bank's Monetary Policy Committee, and it is monitoring them closely. (published 1005 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EU'S ALMUNIA:STRONG EURO A THREAT TO TRADE SURPLUS-REPORT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current strength of the euro paired with the global economic slowdown is a threat to the European trade surplus, European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs Joaquin Almunia told Austrian newspaper Der Standard in an interview published Friday. (published 1001 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE GIEVE: RISK GLOBAL IMBALANCES TRANSFERRED, NOT REDUCED    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fact that the U.S. dollar is falling less rapidly against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of countries like China that have large current account surpluses means there is a risk global imbalances will be transferred, not reduced, Bank of England Deputy Governor John Gieve said Friday. (published 0901 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EU SUMMIT: IMPROVEMENTS NEEDED IN PRUDENTIAL FRAMEWORK -DRAFT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European Union heads of state and government Friday urged banks and financial institutions to boost transparency and improve their prudential margins to allow financial markets to reach stability. (published 1027 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK OF FRANCE CUTS FRENCH 1Q GROWTH TO 0.4% FROM 0.5%    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of France Friday said it lowered its forecast for French economic growth to +0.4% in the first quarter, from an initial estimate of +0.5%, in its February monthly report. (published 0734 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE GIEVE: ASIA TO BE AFFECTED BY US SLOWDOWN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian economies are still growing strongly but will feel the impact of the slowdown in the U.S. and elsewhere, Deputy Governor of the Bank of England John Gieve said Friday. (published 1023 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: SPAIN 07 CURRENT ACCOUNT DEFICIT WIDENS BY 25%    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spain's current account deficit widened by 25% in 2007, partly as a result of rising interest payments to foreign creditors, data from the Bank of Spain showed Friday. (published 0844 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: GERMAN FINAL FEB CPI +0.5% MM, +2.8% YY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German annual consumer price inflation in February was 0.5% on the month and 2.8% on the year, driven by energy prices, final data released by the federal statistics office showed Friday. (published 0712 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =CHINA JAN-FEB URBAN INVESTMENT GROWTH SLOWS VS 2007    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's investment growth slowed slightly in the first two months of the year from a brisk pace in 2007, likely reflecting concerns that a slump in the U.S economy could crimp export and manufacturing growth, analysts said. (published 0651 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: FINNISH FEB CPI +0.5% ON MONTH, +3.7% ON YEAR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finland's consumer price index rose 0.5% on a monthly basis, and was 3.7% higher on a yearly basis in February, data from Statistics Finland showed Friday. (published 0724 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SINGAPORE CENTRAL BK BUYS USD FROM S$1.3795-S$1.3815-TRADERS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Friday to reverse a rise in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , two traders familiar with its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said. (published 0253 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA FINANCE MINISTER: ECON GROWTH TO SLOW TO 8.7% THIS FY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pace of India's economic growth will ease to 8.7% in the current fiscal year, which ends March 31, following a general slowdown in all sectors except electricity, hotels, transport and communications, Finance Minister P. Chidambaram said Friday. (published 0640 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA FIN MIN: CONTROLLING INFLATION GOVT'S TOP PRIORITY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian government and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aim to keep inflation under check, which is its biggest priority, Finance Minister P. Chidambaram said Friday. (published 0610 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par  \par =MIDDAY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : MAJORS CALMER AS DOLLAR CLIMBS A LITTLE As European trading started, it looked like the dollar could be in line for a fresh slump, with the euro hitting a fresh record high at $1.5652, while the dollar dropped to Y99.85 against the yen. (published 1048 GMT)  \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: PROBABLY CAPPED    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-end interest rate futures are likely to retrace lower Friday. (published 0724 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR/USD HITS $1.5652, NEW HIGH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro has made a new all-time high at $1.5652, a move above which is not expected to take place Friday but would have $1.5680 minor resistance in sight. It is more likely that $1.5587 minor support and $1.5522 support get revisited. (published 0753 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVT BOND FUTURES SOAR; 10-YR YIELD NEAR 3-YR LOW ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on 10-year Japanese government bonds plumbed multiyear lows Friday as the yen strengthened sharply against the dollar, prompting investors to pull their money out of stocks to park in bonds. (published 0719 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: SINGAPORE FEB EXPORTS LIKELY DOWN 5.2% ON MONTH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's non-oil domestic exports likely contracted in February due to continued weakness in the electronics sector and a drop in the number of working days. (published 0642 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/14)HEARD ON THE ST:SEC PLAN HAS POTENTIAL FOR FALSE HOPES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Just as companies are closing their books on another tumultuous quarter, regulators are working on a plan that would let them tell investors that things may not be as lousy as they seem. (published 0300 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: S&P'S REASSURANCE IS A BIT RICH, REALLY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets in Asia are breathing a sigh of relief Friday, courtesy of  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 , but take with a grain of salt its prediction that we may be through the worst of it. (published 0022 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/14) CARLYLE FUND IN FREE FALL AS ITS BANKS GET NERVOUS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Carlyle Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the powerful Washington-based buyout firm, has long been known for its gold-plated connections. Its chairman is Louis Gerstner, the legendary former IBM chief, and it once employed both former British Prime Minister John Major and former President George H.W. Bush. (published 0306 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: SLIPPING DOWN THE FISCAL SLOPE IN ASIA    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the global credit market depressed and as the U.S. economy continuing to weaken, taking the rest of the world with it, we are going to see more countries succumb to the temptation to take their credit cards out for a spin. (published 2339 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Vaughn Mckenzie-Landell, Dow Jones Newswires, 020 7842 9273, vaughn.mckenzie-landell@dowjones.com [ 14-03-08 1101GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080314e43e000il\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
