\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Resumes Fall Vs Yen, Breaks Below Y100 Again   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Mochizuki   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 06:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar resumed its downtrend against the yen and fell below Y100 as non-Japanese players sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of their pessimistic view of the U.S. economy and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "The dollar managed to stay above Y100 for most of the Asian hours, but nobody thought the dollar will be supported at that level," said Masashi Matsuzawa, a senior dealer at  \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank.\par \par \uc2 Players are turning their focus to the U.S. consumer price index for February due at 1230 GMT. If it exceeds an average 0.2% increase forecast by economists polled by Dow Jones, the greenback may fall below the 12-year-low of Y99.77 marked overnight and close to Y99.00, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 "Higher inflation data, coupled with the weak spending figures overnight, will prompt players to speculate that U.S. personal consumption will decline," Hideki Amikura, a senior dealer at Nomura Trust and Banking, said.   \par \par \uc2 As of 0530 GMT, the greenback was around Y99.94, lower than Y100.78 New York Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Players said the next big psychological support is Y97.   \par \par \uc2 "The yen's recent surge is a result of dollar weakness," said Jun Kato, a senior dealer at  \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\uc2  "Japan's economic fundamentals aren't so positive."   \par \par \uc2 Other dealers agreed, saying Japanese players such as institutional and amateur investors had already expected the U.S. unit to fall below Y100, suggesting further sharp plunges due to panic-selling are unlikely.   \par \par \uc2 "The moves overnight were a bit aggressive just because players sold a great amount of the dollar in order to break option-related barriers," an option dealer at a non-Japanese bank in Tokyo said. "The dollar will fall, but probably not so rapidly."   \par \par \uc2 Most analysts and strategists in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  concluded that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese government is "very unlikely" for now.   \par \par \uc2 "The government has ample reasons for not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now," said Taisuke Tanaka, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at  \uc2 Lehman Brothers.\uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't stop the dollar's decline anyway. Recovery of U.S. economic fundamentals is what's needed."   \par \par \uc2 And they may be right. Top officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  haven't yet issued clear warnings that it may act soon.   \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the euro breached its all-time high to reach $1.5652, and stood at $1.5648 as of 0530 GMT from $1.5613 New York overnight and Y156.40 from Y157.20 last night. Players said the euro may climb above $1.6 in a month or two, while the unit likely fall against the yen due to global risk aversion movements.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par USD/JPY Yen              100.09-14  100.62-64  -0.53  101.05  100.10  -10.19 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.5625-29  1.5625-29   0.00  1.5629  1.5590   +7.04 \par GBP/USD Sterling         2.0317-21  2.0311-16  +0.03  2.0331  2.0291   +2.25 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.0086-90  1.010