\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan frets as dollar sinks through 100 yen   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs   \par \uc2 862 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 11:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, changes dateline)   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 13 (Reuters) - The dollar sank through 100 yen on Thursday for the first time in more than a decade, prompting Japanese officials to say they are keeping a close eye on the yen's surge that has slammed the stock market this year.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga tweaked his language to show a bit more concern, saying not only that he was carefully watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but repeating that Group of Seven powers agree excessive exchange rate volatility is undesirable.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese Prime Minister Yasuo Fukuda also chimed in to say rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are undesirable.   \par \par \uc2 But analysts played down the risk of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise, despite fears among some that Japan may follow the United States into recession this year.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a long history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to cap the yen and protect exporters. But it has stayed out of the market for four years as the economy has pulled out of its decade-long slump and bout of deflation.   \par \par \uc2 "If they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market ... the upward trend in yen against dollar would not change as long as the fundamentals don't change," said Taisuke Tanaka, who also warned of increasing political pressure to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Nukaga said the dollar's fall reflected the weakness of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than yen strength. Aggressive Federal Reserve interest rate cuts, along with fears of a deep U.S. economic recession, have undermined the dollar this year.   \par \par \uc2 STOCKS TUMBLING   \par \par \uc2 Fear about the impact on corporate profits of a stronger yen has sent the benchmark Nikkei share average tumbling nearly 20 percent this year to a 2-1/2-year low.   \par \par \uc2 The yen's jump was seen posing a further risk to Japanese companies. Futures on the Nikkei fell in Singapore trade while Japanese government bond futures climbed to match a 2-1/2-year high in evening trade   \par \par \uc2 The slide in stocks is greater than that seen in the United States as major exporters are the mainstay of growth in Japan.   \par \par \uc2 "The yen's rise, a decline in the dollar, and rises in oil prices are beginning to have a negative effect on corporate profits," Japanese Economics Minister Hiroko Ota said in parliament.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar slid as low as 99.77 yen breaking below the 100 level for the first time since November 1995. So far this year that U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has tumbled 10 percent against the yen.   \par \par \uc2 Last week the president of  \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2  said the yen had strengthened too much, and analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had climbed to levels that could dent the automaking giant.   \par \par \uc2 "Even Toyota will find it difficult to achieve higher profits with the dollar at 100 yen," said Tatsuo Yoshida, an auto analysts at UBS Securities in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 IMPORTERS BENEFIT   \par \par \uc2 But the strengthening yen has been a boon to Japanese companies importing raw materials because it offsets some of the steep rise in prices, as well as mitigating the impact of oil prices at $110 a barrel.   \par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for  \uc2 Nippon Paper Group\uc2  said that every one yen rise against the dollar increases her firm's operating profit by 700 million yen. But she also acknowledged the risks of a weaker economy.   \par \par \uc2 "In the long run, if the stronger yen causes the domestic economy to slow down, then the paper and pulp industry will also be affected," she said.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said they now expected the dollar to fall to 98 yen and possibly 95 yen in coming weeks.   \par \par \uc2 Yet analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be wary about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  after having stayed out of the market since March 2004 -- the longest Tokyo has gone without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  since at least 1991.   \par \par \uc2 The strong forces behind the dollar's fall mean Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be doomed to fail, analysts say, and the yen strength is largely limited to one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the ailing dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "In the current environment, I think it will be hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to conduct solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Daisaku Ueno, head of the international research unit at Nomura Securities in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 "Even if they were going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  I think it would require cooperation with the United States. But judging from comments from Treasury Secretary Paulson, that does not seem likely to happen very soon," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Compared to when the dollar was last below 100 in late 1995, the yen is some 26 percent weaker when compared with a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Japan's trading partners   \par \par \uc2 Japan spent a whopping 35 trillion yen ($350 billion) in 2003 and the first quarter of 2004 to fight against the yen's rise against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 In 1995, the dollar fell to an all-time low near 80 yen as the heated trade dispute between the Japan and the United States led to heightened pressure for a stronger yen. (Additional reporting by Yuzo Saeki, Leika Kihara, Masayuki Kitano, Sumio Ito, Nobuhiro Kobu and Nathan Layne) ($1=100 Yen)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YEN (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080313e43d000lr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
