\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ Official: BOJ Has 'Never, Ever' Considered Setting Up SWF   \b0\par\par\uc2 381 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 12:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan has never considered setting up a sovereign wealth fund to invest Japan's burgeoning \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, an official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since March 2004, but its reserves have steadily increased at an annual rate of return of around 4% over recent years, Toshio Idesawa, Director General of the international department at the BOJ, said in a speech in London.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are second only to China, and last month breached the $1 trillion mark.   \par \par \uc2 "Money makes money and we simply let it be," Idesawa said. While some have jumped at the idea of Japan setting up a sovereign wealth fund with its reserves, "the Bank of Japan has never, ever even considered the idea," he said.   \par \par \uc2 He stressed that unlike the sovereign wealth funds of oil-producing countries or nations with fiscal surpluses, Japan's foreign reserves are neither wealth, nor savings, but assets that are fully financed by debt. That makes it very risky to invest them in volatile assets.   \par \par \uc2 Some lawmakers in the ruling Liberal Democratic Party are keen on creating a fund, arguing that the government should seek higher returns from those funds for rebuilding fiscal health and providing for other national projects.   \par \par \uc2 The LDP has launched a team to explore the possibility of establishing a state-backed investment fund, but \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have questioned the virtue of a government meddling in free-market activity through an investment fund.   \par \par \uc2 Idesawa indicated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  planned to take some measures to free up the way it invests its assets.   \par \par \uc2 "Within the mandate I am given by the board, in the coming months I will take some steps with caution to widen the variety of our portfolio and to rebalance slightly the constraints imposed upon the way of investment," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Idesawa also said that the BOJ was "fairly comfortable" with the current amount and portfolio of the foreign assets it holds," adding "we may well continue to let our assets grow a little more."   \par \par \uc2 -By Natasha Brereton, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 20 7842 9254; natasha.brereton@dowjones.com [ 13-03-08 1340GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080313e43d000mu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
