\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar Below Y100 Not Setting Off Alarm Bells   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,147 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 18:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Dan Molinski \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--A change in a big round number sometimes leads policymakers to stand up and act on an issue, but the dollar's drop Thursday below the symbolic 100 level against the yen didn't seem to do the trick.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Investors looking for strong words from U.S. officials to counter the dollar's descent to levels not seen against the yen in 13 years saw their hopes quickly dashed. In a speech Thursday morning in Washington, just hours after the dollar hit Y99.77, Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson gave no indication that the breach of the Y100 level had raised any red flags.   \par \par \uc2 He reiterated the line he's been repeating for months that the strong dollar is in the U.S. interests. He added, in response to a question, that "the long-term fundamentals are strong and I believe it's going to be reflected in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2 Those who think the administration should change its stance of benign neglect argue that further steep declines could lead to a global loss of confidence in the dollar. That, in turn, could spur foreign investors to reduce their enormous holdings of U.S. Treasury bonds, a vital government funding source. Another worry would be that, after six years of steady dollar declines, investors may begin to demand a premium on their dollar assets to make up for the weak exchange rate, which would be costly for the U.S. government and further harm the already embattled economy.   \par \par \uc2 But Paulson's underlying message was: The U.S. government remains comfortable with the dollar's current exchange rate values, and may even want to see it weaken a bit more to boost exports, reduce the widening trade deficit and help kick-start its sluggish economy.   \par \par \uc2 It makes a lot of sense in the current environment. U.S. officials are not going to try to talk the dollar higher with verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the very moment the Federal Reserve is pulling the dollar lower with interest rate cut after interest rate cut.   \par \par \uc2 "No \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is always better than failed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Chris Turner, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING in London. "The U.S., it seems, is saving its ammunition for when the markets will be more receptive" to calls for a stronger dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Thursday afternoon, the dollar was at Y100.71, down from Y101.73, while the euro was at $1.5599, up from $1.5557 late Wednesday. The euro was at Y156.88, down from Y158.25, according to EBS. The U.K. pound was at $2.0305, up from $2.0273, and the dollar was at CHF1.0124, off from CHF1.0140 Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    The Yen Not Cheap, Nor Expensive \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In some respects, the dollar's drop to a 13-year trough against the yen Thursday was seen as even more significant than the greenback's record declines against the euro in recent days.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After all, the 15-nation euro has been in existence for less than a decade, so there's little historical perspective with which to compare exchange rates. The yen, meanwhile, has been around a good many years, so a sharp drop against Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could create concern that the dollar's normal trading bands are at risk of ripping apart.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts point out that the dollar at Y100 is actually a very fair value when one looks at any one of a myriad of indexes that track where \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates should be when factors such as inflation are included.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategist Bankim Chadha says he's not at all surprised the U.S. is shrugging off the dollar's move below Y100.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The dollar versus yen's fair value is 100, so right now it's neither cheap nor expensive," Chadha said. "While the dollar's move lower has been rather quick, and the 100 level is rather important on a symbolic level ... the message from the Fed and the Treasury remains clear - they believe the dollar going down is helpful."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As such, it seems that concern about any actual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Fed in the near term to prop up the dollar, by entering the market to buy greenbacks, is rather remote.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts also said they don't expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to directly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  either, at least at the current levels.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We think the MoF may well \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in roundabout ways to manage the pace of yen appreciation, really to keep the market liquid and two-way pricing alive," said Ray Farris, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse.\f28 \uc2 \f2  "But we do not expect it to try to defend aggressively Y100 or even levels below that down to perhaps Y90-95."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    What About Bush? \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Putting something of a wrinkle in the U.S.'s apparent comfort with current exchange rate levels on the dollar were comments from U.S. President George W. Bush during an interview Wednesday night on public television.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Responding to a question about whether he'd like to see a stronger dollar, he said "absolutely."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While his comments were no doubt closely aligned to those of Paulson, analysts were quick to note that by acknowledging he'd like to see a stronger dollar, he was saying that he wasn't happy with current levels.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bush went on to suggest ways to make the dollar stronger.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We can send signals to the world that their capital is welcomed into the United States, that we'll fight off protectionism, and that we'll deal with this - you know, the dollar was strengthened when people realized the relative strength of our economy," Bush said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But at the end of the day, what Bush says in terms of exchange rates may not carry the same weight as remarks made by Paulson or Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Paulson certainly didn't back up what Bush said, and had nothing new to offer from what he's said before," ING's Turner said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And the White House followed up Thursday, with spokesman Tony Fratto insisting Bush's comments didn't reflect a change in government policy toward the dollar.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Dan Molinski covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He was previously a correspondent for Dow Jones in Bogota.)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dan Molinski; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2245; dan.molinski@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Henry Pulizzi in Washington contributed to this report.)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAme