\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 936 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 09:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN ECON MIN OTA: 4Q GDP GROWTH FIGURE RELATIVELY HIGH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economy Minister Hiroko Ota said Wednesday revised gross domestic growth figures for the fourth quarter last year were relatively healthy, but warned that downside risks to the Japanese economy are increasing. (published 0719 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =REVISED 4Q JAPAN GDP FIRM, BUT WORRIES AHEAD    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government said Wednesday that the economy grew at a slightly slower pace than initially reported in the final quarter of 2007, but that economic conditions remained firm. (published 0608 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVT: FEB CONSUMER SENTIMENT 36.1 VS 37.5 JAN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer sentiment in Japan worsened in February from the previous month, falling for the fifth straight month, the government said Wednesday. (published 0508 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MINUTES HINT BOJ MORE WORRIED ABOUT JAPAN ECON IN FEB    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan officials at February's policy meeting were more worried about a sliding U.S. economy and some weak corners of the domestic economy, meeting minutes showed Tuesday, indicating their wavering confidence. (published 0140 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =BOJ NODA: RISKS REMAIN FOR JAPAN'S ECON GROWTH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weak industrial production threatens Japan's economic growth, but the Bank of Japan is unlikely to cut rates to boost the economy, a BOJ board member suggested Wednesday. (published 0641 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =SAKAKIBARA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WON'T \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WITH DLR OVER Y90    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar is likely to fall below the key level of Y100 in coming weeks, but the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  probably won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets unless it falls below Y90, former Japanese Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara said Wednesday. (published 0251 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par *FRENCH FEB CPI +0.2% ON MONTH; +2.8% ON YEAR (published 0745 GMT)  \par  \par  \par JAPAN FEB CORPORATE BANKRUPTCY CASES UP 14.3% ON YEAR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese bankruptcy cases rose 14.3% on year in February to 935 cases, private credit research agency Teikoku Databank Ltd. said Wednesday. (published 0400 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par *CHINA COMMERCE MIN: YUAN RISE HELPS IMPORTS (published 0723 GMT)  \par  \par  \par =CHINA JAN-FEB RETAIL SALES, ACTUAL FDI SURGE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's retail sales strengthened in the first two months of 2008 from a year earlier, as consumers spent more on meat and cooking oil due to higher prices in recent months. (published 0540 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par *CHINA: HIGH JAN, FEB CPI MAKE IT HARD TO CURB 08 PRICE RISE (published 0652 GMT)  \par  \par  \par JAPAN RULING PARTIES SET BOJ CHIEF LOWER HOUSE VOTE DATE-KYODO    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's ruling parties have agreed upon March 13 as the date when the Lower House will vote on Toshiro Muto's nomination to become head of the Bank of Japan, Kyodo News reported. (published 0622 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =JAPAN'S NUKAGA SAYS GOVT STILL FAVORS MUTO AS BOJ GOV    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister hinted Wednesday the government will make one more push to make Bank of Japan Deputy Gov. Toshiro Muto the next chief of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0312 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =AUSTRALIAN CONSUMERS GLOOMY; POLICY FORECASTS CHANGE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Confidence among Australian consumers has weakened sharply in March to its lowest level since 1993, sparking forecasts from economists that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to further tighten monetary policy. (published 0201 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FEB DOMESTIC CGPI +3.4% ON YR VS +3.0% IN JAN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's domestic corporate goods prices rose 3.4% in February from the same month a year earlier, the Bank of Japan said Wednesday, marking the 48th straight month of rises on the back of strong international commodity prices. (published 0015 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: INDIA JAN INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT +5.3% ON YEAR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's industrial output grew 5.3% on year in January, compared with a 7.7% rise in the previous month, government data issued Wednesday showed. (published 0634 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA JAN-FEB VEHICLE SALES UP 19.3% ON YR TO 1.52 MLN UNITS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's vehicle sales rose 19.3% in the first two months of 2008 from a year earlier to 1.52 million units, data from a semi-official industry group showed. (published 0700 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: DOLLAR STILL TRYING TO RECOVER    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: The euro is losing upside momentum, but might still revisit Monday's $1.5407 inside day high. In case of a move above it, the all-time high at $1.5465 will be in focus again. Minor support is around $1.5331 and at $1.5312, that's Monday's inside day low. A fall through this level will have $1.5276 support in sight. (published 0741 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BUNDS, GILTS CAPPED    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-end interest rate futures are short-term capped and may retest Tuesday's lows Wednesday. (published 0705 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/12) UPDATE: FED OFFERS LIFELINE FOR SPURNED DEBT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve delivered a shot in the arm to stressed financial markets by offering to take a big chunk of now difficult-to-trade securities temporarily out of circulation. (published 0304 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FED WATCH:WITH EACH STEP, FED INCHES NEAR MORAL-HAZARD LINE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With each step the U.S. Federal Reserve takes to alleviate the credit crunch, it inches ever closer to the dreaded B-word: bailout. (published 1904 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: BEN BERNANKE IS NOT YOUR FAIRY GODMOTHER    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  liquidity measures around the globe will be a short-term salve for financial markets, but they are not a magical key which will unlock credit markets. (published 2351 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: ROAD TO RECOVERY LIES IN MORE TRANSPARENCY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So much has gone wrong with complicated financial instruments that it's hard to know where to begin fixing the problems. (published 1915 GMT) [ 12-03-08 0807GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080312e43c000bw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
