\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 936 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 06:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE:JAPAN OCT-DEC ANNUALIZED GDP REVISED DN; CAPEX WEAKER    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government Wednesday revised down the annualized economic growth rate for the final three months of 2007 largely because of weaker business investment. (published 0057 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FEB DOMESTIC CGPI +3.4% ON YR VS +3.0% IN JAN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's domestic corporate goods prices rose 3.4% in February from the same month a year earlier, the Bank of Japan said Wednesday, marking the 48th straight month of rises on the back of strong international commodity prices. (published 0015 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA JANUARY-FEBRUARY RETAIL SALES UP 20.2% ON YEAR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's retail sales rose 20.2% in the first two months of this year from the year-earlier period, the government said Wednesday. (published 0212 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: AUSTRALIAN CONSUMERS GLOOMY; POLICY FORECASTS CHANGE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Confidence among Australian consumers has weakened sharply in March to its lowest level since 1993, sparking forecasts from economists that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to further tighten monetary policy. (published 0201 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:MINUTES HINT BOJ MORE WORRIED ABOUT JAPAN ECON IN FEB    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan officials at February's policy meeting were more worried about a sliding U.S. economy and some weak corners of the domestic economy, meeting minutes showed Tuesday, indicating their wavering confidence. (published 0140 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: BOJ NODA: NO NEED TO CHANGE MONETARY POLICY PATH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan won't dramatically alter its monetary policy stance despite slowing economic growth, BOJ board member Tadao Noda said Wednesday, damping market hopes for an interest-rate cut. (published 0333 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:JAPAN'S NUKAGA SAYS GOVT STILL FAVORS MUTO AS BOJ GOV    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister hinted Wednesday the government will make one more push to make Bank of Japan Deputy Gov. Toshiro Muto the next chief of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0312 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: SAKAKIBARA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WON'T \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WITH DLR OVER Y90    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar is likely to fall below the key level of Y100 in coming weeks, but the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  probably won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets unless it falls below Y90, former Japanese Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara said Wednesday. (published 0251 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FEB CORPORATE BANKRUPTCY CASES UP 14.3% ON YEAR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese bankruptcy cases rose 14.3% on year in February to 935 cases, private credit research agency Teikoku Databank Ltd. said Wednesday. (published 0400 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA FEBRUARY ACTUAL FDI US$6.93 BLN; UP 38.3% ON YEAR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China attracted US$6.93 billion in actual foreign direct investment in February, up 38.3% from a year earlier, the Ministry of Commerce said Wednesday. (published 0241 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA MIN: SOVEREIGN FUND RULES SHOULD BE SET MULTILATERALLY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Foreign Minister Yang Jiechi said Wednesday a code of conduct for sovereign wealth funds ought to be set multilaterally. (published 0358 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PLANS TO ESTABLISH FOUR PRIVATE-EQUITY FUNDS - REPORT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China plans to establish four private-equity funds to provide financing for the infrastructure and manufacturing sectors, Caijing Magazine reported late Tuesday. (published 0159 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FEB SEASONALLY ADJUSTED JOBLESS AT 3.0%, SAME AS JAN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's seasonally adjusted jobless rate was at 3.0% in February, unchanged from the preceding month, the National Statistical Office said Wednesday. (published 0443 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN LEADING EMPLOYMENT INDICATOR FALLS IN MARCH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Department of Employment said Wednesday that its leading indicator of employment fell to a negative 0.055 in March from a revised negative 0.048 in February. (published 0323 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: NZ FEB HOUSE PRICES EASE; MKT TO WEAKEN FURTHER    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's national average house price fell in February and annual price inflation was almost flat, providing further evidence the housing market is slowing, a survey released Wednesday showed. (published 0140 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ING NZ SUSPENDS WITHDRAWALS FROM 2 FUNDS; CREDIT CRUNCH BITES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fund manager ING New Zealand said Wednesday it has suspended withdrawals from two of its funds due to the effects of the global credit crunch on financial markets. (published 0051 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par KIRIN HOLDINGS PRICES Y200B OF STRAIGHT BONDS IN 4 TRANCHES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kirin Holdings Co. offered Y200 billion of straight corporate bonds Wednesday via four tranches, said lead manager Mitsubishi UFJ Securities. (published 0150 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par KOREA-US RATE GAP UNLIKELY TO BOOST KOREAN WON -BOK ECONOMIST    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A senior economist at the Bank of Korea said Wednesday that the widening differential between U.S. and South Korean interest rates is unlikely to spark any large capital inflows into the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  until risk aversion eases and the stock market starts to recover. (published 0327 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/12) UPDATE: FED OFFERS LIFELINE FOR SPURNED DEBT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve delivered a shot in the arm to stressed financial markets by offering to take a big chunk of now difficult-to-trade securities temporarily out of circulation. (published 0304 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FED WATCH:WITH EACH STEP, FED INCHES NEAR MORAL-HAZARD LINE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With each step the U.S. Federal Reserve takes to alleviate the credit crunch, it inches ever closer to the dreaded B-word: bailout. (published 1904 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: BEN BERNANKE IS NOT YOUR FAIRY GODMOTHER    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  liquidity measures around the globe will be a short-term salve for financial markets, but they are not a magical key which will unlock credit markets. (published 2351 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: ROAD TO RECOVERY LIES IN MORE TRANSPARENCY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So much has gone wrong with complicated financial instruments that it's hard to know where to begin fixing the problems. (published 1915 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-03-08 0501GMT ] 