\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,150 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 03:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE:JAPAN OCT-DEC ANNUALIZED GDP REVISED DN; CAPEX WEAKER    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government Wednesday revised down the annualized economic growth rate for the final three months of 2007 largely because of weaker business investment. (published 0057 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FEB DOMESTIC CGPI +3.4% ON YR VS +3.0% IN JAN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's domestic corporate goods prices rose 3.4% in February from the same month a year earlier, the Bank of Japan said Wednesday, marking the 48th straight month of rises on the back of strong international commodity prices. (published 0015 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE:MINUTES HINT BOJ MORE WORRIED ABOUT JAPAN ECON IN FEB    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan officials at February's policy meeting were more worried about a sliding U.S. economy and some weak corners of the domestic economy, meeting minutes showed Tuesday, indicating their wavering confidence. (published 0140 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN UPPER HOUSE VOTES DOWN BOJ GOV CANDIDATE MUTO    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Upper House of Parliament on Wednesday voted down the government's proposal to promote Bank of Japan Deputy Gov. Toshiro Muto to governor, making a leadership vacancy at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  even more likely. (published 0116 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EX-MOF SAKAKIBARA:DLR TO BREAK Y100, NO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OVER Y90    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar is likely to fall below the key level of Y100 in coming weeks, but the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  probably won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets unless it falls below Y90, former Japanese Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara said Wednesday. (published 0140 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par KIRIN HOLDINGS PRICES Y200B OF STRAIGHT BONDS IN 4 TRANCHES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kirin Holdings Co. offered Y200 billion of straight corporate bonds Wednesday via four tranches, said lead manager Mitsubishi UFJ Securities. (published 0150 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PLANS TO ESTABLISH FOUR PRIVATE-EQUITY FUNDS - REPORT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China plans to establish four private-equity funds to provide financing for the infrastructure and manufacturing sectors, Caijing Magazine reported late Tuesday. (published 0159 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN CONSUMER SENTIMENT INDEX -9.1% IN MAR VS FEB    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An index of consumer sentiment in Australia fell 9.1% in March from February as aggressive interest rate hikes by the Reserve Bank of Australia pushed household budgets closer to breaking point. (published 2330 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: NZ FEB HOUSE PRICES EASE; MKT TO WEAKEN FURTHER    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's national average house price fell in February and annual price inflation was almost flat, providing further evidence the housing market is slowing, a survey released Wednesday showed. (published 0140 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ING NZ SUSPENDS WITHDRAWALS FROM 2 FUNDS; CREDIT CRUNCH BITES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fund manager ING New Zealand said Wednesday it has suspended withdrawals from two of its funds due to the effects of the global credit crunch on financial markets. (published 0051 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =AT A GLANCE: FED EXPANDS SECURITIES LENDING PROGRAM    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve Tuesday announced an expansion of its securities lending program, saying it will lend up to $200 billion of Treasury securities to primary dealers. (published 1625 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ECB TO PROVIDE USD LIQUIDITY IN CONJUCTION WITH FED    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday that it will resume its provision of dollar liquidity to stem reemerging pressure in the funding markets. (published 1313 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TSY PAULSON: HOUSING CONTINUES TO POSE BIGGEST ECONOMIC RISK    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ongoing housing crisis continues to pose the biggest risk to the U.S. economy and will take some time to play out, Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson said Tuesday. (published 1527 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TSY PAULSON: US FINANCIAL FIRMS NEED TO FOCUS ON CAPITAL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial firms need to ensure they have enough capital to weather the current market storm, Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson said Tuesday. (published 1538 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US TREASURY'S KIMMITT: GROWTH RISKS TO THE DOWNSIDE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fundamentals of the U.S. economy are sound, although there are downside risks to growth, U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Robert Kimmitt said Tuesday. (published 1911 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US JAN TRADE GAP UP LESS THAN EXPECTED    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. trade deficit rose in January as the price for oil set a new record, but the shortfall was smaller than expected, with exports making their largest climb in six months. (published 1230 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ABC/WASH POST: US CONSUMER CONFIDENCE UP 4 PTS LATEST WK    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. overall consumer confidence rose last week, according to an ABC News/Washington Post poll released Tuesday. The consumer comfort index rose 4 points to -30 in the week ended March 9, from -34 a week earlier. (published 2100 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US REP FRANK: BOND INSURERS HAVE MONTH TO RIGHT THE MARKET    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond insurers and rating agencies have to act now to quell the turmoil in the bond markets or Congress will have to step in, House Financial Services Chairman Barney Frank said Tuesday. (published 2046 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par TREASURYS SENT REELING AFTER US FED ANNOUNCES LIQUIDITY PLAN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. government bond prices fell hard Tuesday after the Federal Reserve announced new liquidity measures for Wall Street that sent stock prices shooting upward. (published 2007 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR MAKES BROAD GAINS AFTER FED ACTION    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar made its sharpest one-day gain so far this year against the euro and rose against other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Tuesday after the Federal Reserve announced new efforts to inject liquidity into strained credit markets. (published 2030 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RATE FUTURES REPORT: PRICES FALL HARD ON FED LIQUIDITY MOVE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fear of risk-taking and the surge in rate-cut expectations so evident in recent weeks came to a grinding halt Tuesday in short- and long-term U.S. interest rate futures. (published 2013 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FED WATCH:WITH EACH STEP, FED INCHES NEAR MORAL-HAZARD LINE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With each step the U.S. Federal Reserve takes to alleviate the credit crunch, it inches ever closer to the dreaded B-word: bailout. (published 1904 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: BEN BERNANKE IS NOT YOUR FAIRY GODMOTHER    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  liquidity measures around the globe will be a short-term salve for financial markets, but they are not a magical key which will unlock credit markets. (published 2351 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: ROAD TO RECOVERY LIES IN MORE TRANSPARENCY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So much has gone wrong with complicated financial instruments that it's hard to know where to begin fixing the problems. (published 1915 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =TAKING STOCK: OIL LEAVES FUNDAMENTALS IN THE DUST    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil markets have the distinct aura of one of those passionate but cynical affairs that everyone knows won't last. (published 0045 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =OFF THE RUN: REPO MARKET REMAINS GLOOMY DESPITE FED GIFT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Participants in the securities repurchase market have yet to jump for joy over the Federal Reserve's latest surprise aimed at reviving stalled markets. (published 1824 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VIEW: DOLLAR GAINS ON FED MOVE SHOW RATES STILL RULE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While there is some skepticism over the Federal Reserve's latest plan to boost liquidity, at least it points to smaller Fed rate cuts next week and that's about all that matters for the dollar. (published 1638 GMT) [ 12-03-08 0201GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080312e43c0001y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
