\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,351 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 05:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE:JAPAN JAN CORE MACHINERY ORDERS UP 19.6% ON MONTH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese core machinery orders rose for the first time in three months and marked their biggest rise in more than seven years in January, the government said Monday. (published 0204 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OFFICIALS GIVE NO HINTS OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the yen's surge last week to multiyear highs versus the dollar, Japan's finance minister and his top lieutenant Monday didn't go beyond repeating that they're watchful of the exchange rate, suggesting yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't on the table yet. (published 0305 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 'S SENIOR DPJ MEMBERS STILL AGAINST MUTO'S BOJ PROMOTION    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Senior officials of Japan's biggest opposition party sounded negative about the official candidate for the next Bank of Japan governor, heightening the possibility that the nominee would meet parliamentary blockage and fail to assume the post. (published 2252 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA FEBRUARY PPI UP 6.6% ON YEAR VS UP 6.1% IN JANUARY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's producer price index rose 6.6% on year in February, accelerating for a seventh straight month and highlighting the inflation problem plaguing China, data released by the National Bureau of Statistics showed Monday. (published 0211 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL:NEW LIMITS FOR BK SHORT-TERM FOREIGN DEBT QUOTA    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will further limit banks' foreign short-term debt quota and is pushing ahead with a plan to let mainland residents invest directly in Hong Kong shares, the bank's Tianjin branch head said Sunday. (published 0338 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL: TO RAISE BK RESERVE RATIOS TO CURB INFLATION    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  will rely more on raising the reserve ratio requirements of banks than on hiking interest rates in the fight against inflation, the PBOC Nanjing branch head said Sunday. (published 0408 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MAS SURVEY: ECONOMISTS EXPECT SINGAPORE 2008 GDP +5.6%    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A quarterly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  survey issued Monday predicts Singapore's economy will grow 5.6% in 2008, slowing from last year's pace as external demand softens. (published 0400 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FUKUDA:NO PLAN TO PROPOSE ANOTHER BOJ CHIEF CANDIDATE-KYODO    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Yasuo Fukuda said Monday he has no plan to propose an alternative candidate for the head of the Bank of Japan, Kyodo News reported. (published 0358 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA OFFICIAL:TIME NOT GOOD FOR CROSS-SECTOR FINCL REGULATOR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The time is not yet right for China to set up a cross-sector financial regulator, a senior official said Sunday, dismissing recent speculation about Beijing creating such an agency in a widely-anticipated government restructuring. (published 1025 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FEB PRODUCER PRICES +1.1% ON MONTH, +6.8% ON YEAR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's February producer price inflation accelerated to its highest on-year level in more than three years due largely to a sharp rise in global oil and raw materials prices, the Bank of Korea said Monday. (published 0300 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA GOVT TARGETS 6% GDP GROWTH IN 2008 VS +4.9% IN 2007    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government released its "action plan" for 2008 aiming to achieve around 6% economic growth, higher than last year's 4.9% rise, through across-the-board tax cuts to spur corporate investment while suppressing inflationary pressure, a report released by the Ministry of Strategy and Finance Monday showed. (published 0000 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NZ HOUSE PRICES CONTINUE TO COOL IN FEB,ON-YR GROWTH SLOWS-QV    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand house-price inflation continued to cool in February, signaling a sustained period of soft activity, according to a survey released Sunday. (published 1059 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM PLANS TO UP FOREIGN STAKE CAP IN UNLISTED COS - EXEC    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam plans to raise the cap on foreign ownership in unlisted public companies to 40% from the current 30% to boost foreign investment in the local market, a government official said Monday. (published 0352 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MALAYSIA PM ABDULLAH AHMAD BADAWI SWORN IN FOR NEW 5-YR TERM    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's prime minister took the oath of office for a new five-year term Monday, rejecting calls to resign after an unprecedented electoral setback that has shaken the country's political landscape. (published 0317 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: SPANISH PM THANKS VOTERS FOR `CLEAR VICTORY'    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spanish Prime Minister Jose Luis Rodriguez Zapatero won re-election Sunday, as voters dismissed worries about a slumping economy, immigration and resurgent Basque separatists to hand him a second term, in a day of voting overshadowed by the militants' killing of a member of his party. (published 0037 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE:EARLY RESULTS:SMALL LEFTIST LEAD IN FRENCH LOCAL VOTE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Partial results showed President Nicolas Sarkozy's backers losing in Paris and other key French cities Sunday in the first round of municipal elections seen as a referendum on the increasingly unpopular conservative. (published 0055 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =WSJA: PAKISTAN WINNING PARTIES SEAL POWER-SHARING AGREEMENT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pakistan's two main political parties - collective winners of last month's national elections - sealed a power-sharing deal Sunday, increasing pressure on embattled President Pervez Musharraf. (published 1842 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: MALAYSIAN VOTERS RING CHANGES IN GOVT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The shock result from Malaysia's general elections on Saturday is positive for the nation's social and economic development over the long run. In the shorter term, however, the outlook is decidedly cloudier. (published 0039 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO KEEP RATES STEADY - POLL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to leave its benchmark interest rate unchanged when it meets later this week due to a rapid rise in consumer prices, economists said Monday. (published 0148 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =US MONEY WEEK AHEAD: NEW TALE FROM JAPAN: BUY, NOT SELL TSYS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a break from tradition, Japanese investors aren't selling Treasurys and other non-yen bonds these days even as the fiscal year-end is approaching. (published 2200 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =US DATA WEEK AHEAD:FEB CPI, RETAIL SALES TOP DATA LINEUP    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After the past week's barrage of weaker figures culminated in another plunge in employment, economists aren't expecting stellar news in the coming week when it comes to the numbers. (published 2230 GMT Friday)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FOCUS:PBOC TAKES AIM AT HOT MONEY,BUT FINDING TARGET TRICKY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China increasingly is blaming hot money for a plethora of economic ailments and has pledged to cut the inflow - but the task won't be easy. (published 1142 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =TAKING STOCK: EXTERNAL PROPS COULD FAIL GERMANY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Plenty of commentary, including on occasion this column, has tried to wring some comfort for battered equity investors from emerging-market prospects. (published 0045 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FOCUS:FRENCH VOTERS SANCTION GOVT IN 1ST ROUND OF LOCAL VOTE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After nine months in power, French President Nicolas Sarkozy on Sunday got a first warning from voters, as the Leftist opposition was propelled ahead of the governing party in the first round of local elections. (published 2206 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: JUST HOW BAD IS THE CREDIT CRUNCH FOR THE US?    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A recent paper by a group of academic, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and industry economists - "Lessons from the Mortgage Market Meltdown," Greenlaw, Hatzius, Kashyap, Shin," - tries to quantify the impact. Their conclusions don't make for pretty reading. (published 0045 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEDGE FUNDS, PVT EQUITY PRESSED ON RISK MGMT AS RETURNS FALL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Institutional investors are pressing the managers of alternative assets, including private equity and hedge funds, on issues of transparency and risk management, as returns come down and governance becomes as important as performance,  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PricewaterhouseCoopers\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Monday. (published 0001 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS LOWER AS RECESSION FEARS CHIP AWAY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recession fears mushroomed Friday, pushing stocks sharply lower and prices of long-dated Treasury securities higher following a bleak jobs report and a warning by  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thornburg Mortgage\f28 \uc2 \f2  that there is "substantial doubt" about its ability to stay afloat. (published 2039 GMT Friday)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/10) CREDIT MARKETS: FEAR ENSNARES STRUCTURED PRODUCTS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rational thinking has gone out the window in the credit markets. (published 2330 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/10)COMMODITIES REPORT:ANALYSTS ADJUST CRUDE PREDICTIONS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A two-week string of record oil prices has left prognosticators at banks and brokerage firms struggling to keep pace. (published 2330 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-03-08 0401GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080310e43a0007x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
