\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,047 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 10:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =JAPAN CAPEX SHRINKS AT FASTEST PACE IN 5 YEARS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's business investment shrank at its fastest pace in more than five years during the October-December period, government data showed, adding to evidence that the world's number-two economy is heading for a slowdown. (published 0453 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UK CONSUMER CONFIDENCE HITS FRESH RECORD LOW IN FEBRUARY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. consumer confidence sank to a fresh record low in February as consumers' assessment of current economic conditions weakened sharply, data from U.K. lender Nationwide released Wednesday indicated.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (published 0001 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par STERLING FALLS TO NEW LOW AGAINST EUR AT 0.7686 - EBS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (published 0750 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FRENCH BUDGET MINISTER: EURO TOO STRONG AT CURRENT LEVELS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French Budget Minister Eric Woerth said Wednesday that the euro is too strong at current levels. (published 0733 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN FEB SERVICES PMI 52.2 VS 49.2 IN JAN -SOURCES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (published 0855 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FRENCH FEB SERVICES PMI 58.2 VS JAN 56.6 - SOURCES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (published 0850 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ITALIAN FEBRUARY SERVICES PMI 47.2 VS 47.9 IN JAN - SOURCES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The purchasing managers index for Italy's services sector fell to 47.2 in February from 47.9 in January, market sources said Wednesday. A reading above 50.0 indicates the sector is expanding, while a reading below 50.0 signals it is contracting. The decline in the PMI was unexpected. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires last week forecast the PMI would rise to 49.5. (published 0849 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CLINTON WINS SHIFT MOMENTUM; MCCAIN GAINS GOP NOD    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With critical wins in Texas and Ohio on Tuesday, Hillary Clinton broke Barack Obama's momentum in the run for the Democratic presidential nomination. John McCain, meanwhile, cemented the Republican nomination with a string of primary victories. (published 0559 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MCCAIN PREVIEWS KEY ECONOMIC THEMES FOR FALL CAMPAIGN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sen. John McCain, the Republican Party's presumptive nominee for president, on Tuesday previewed fall campaign themes against Democrats by firmly embracing global trade and less regulation. (published 0349 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =CHINA PM WEN: AIM FOR GDP GROWTH OF 8.0% IN 2008    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao pledged Wednesday to cap inflation at 4.8% this year and signaled Beijing will continue trying to steer China toward an orderly economic slowdown. (published 0507 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =AUSTRALIA GDP SLOWS SLIGHTLY BUT DEMAND WHITE HOT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy slowed slightly in the fourth quarter due to sluggish export growth, but domestic demand remained white hot, affirming the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's move to aggressively raise interest rates since mid-2007. (published 0438 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PBOC GOV: NO PLAN TO RAISE INTEREST RATE WEDNESDAY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has no plan to raise interest rates Wednesday, People's  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan said. (published 0406 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA BK REGULATOR: CHINA WILL NOT HAVE SUBPRIME CRISIS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China won't have a subprime mortgage crisis like the one in the U.S. that has rocked global markets because it has no zero downpayment mortgages, the nation's top banking regulator said Wednesday. (published 0423 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA FIN MIN: EXPECT ECONOMY TO GROW OVER 8.8% NEXT FY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's economy is expected to grow over 8.8% in the next fiscal year starting April 1, Finance Minister P. Chidambaram said Wednesday. (published 0439 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: EURO WILL STILL RISE DESPITE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FEARS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The change in European rhetoric towards the euro may well slow the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's advance but it probably won't stop it. (published 0804 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: LONG-END TO BE CAPPED    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-end interest rate futures should be capped and retrace lower Wednesday. (published 0712 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR/GBP TO MAKE NEW HIGH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro is likely to retrace towards $1.5150 minor support, provided $1.5250 minor resistance caps. (published 0759 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PRECIOUS METALS: SPOT GOLD RETREATS IN ASIA, MORE LOSSES SEEN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold fell back into negative territory in Asia Wednesday on follow-through selling from funds and speculators, giving up early gains on bargain hunting, and traders expect further selling pressure during London trading. (published 0650 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: MALAYSIA JAN EXPORTS LIKELY +6.7% ON YR, IPI +6.2%    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's exports likely improved appreciably in January as higher commodity prices offset weaker electronics shipments, economists said. (published 0514 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =POINT OF VIEW: TWO FED OFFICIALS AND TWO INFLATION VIEWS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The key to the U.S. Federal Reserve's success in helping prop the economy through lower interest rates even as inflation stays higher than desired is to manage people's long-term view of inflation. (published 2116 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW:TAIWAN FEB EXPORT VALUE SEEN LOWEST IN YR ON HOLIDAY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's exports value in February likely fell to a year-low as the Chinese New Year holidays cut the number of working days and because of slower demand from the U.S. and Europe. (published 0239 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WON'T \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , BARRING UPHEAVAL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar swoon means \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves are again front-page news in Japan, but as long as the yen rise remains orderly, don't expect authorities to resort to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. (published 2345 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VIEW: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO PROP DOLLAR VS EURO UNLIKELY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European officials can jawbone all they want about the euro's sharp rise against the dollar, but until U.S. officials start agreeing with them, the probability of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains low. (published 2026 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJA(3/5) DOLLAR'S TUMBLE PINCHES JAPAN'S EXPORTERS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's biggest exporters are feeling the pressure of the weaker dollar, raising the prospects that their earnings might finally take a big hit in coming quarters after years of solid growth. (published 2131 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/5) NO ANSWERS YET TO A TRILLION-DOLLAR QUESTION    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turmoil continues in the municipal-bond market. This week tax-free municipals have been yielding more than taxable Treasurys, a rare anomaly. Auctions of variable-rate municipals have been faltering. (published 0056 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =IN THE MONEY: SOVEREIGN FUNDS LET $30B RIDE ON CITI, MERRILL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sovereign wealth funds that recently plowed billions of dollars into major U.S. financial institutions so far are living anything but the "American Dream." (published 2107 GMT) [ 05-03-08 0904GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080305e435000i3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
