\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par *CLINTON IS PROJECTED TO WIN TEXAS DEMOCRATIC PRIMARY - FOX (published 0547 GMT)  \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: JAPAN CAPEX SHRINKS AT FASTEST PACE IN 5 YEARS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's business investment shrank at its fastest pace in more than five years during the October-December period, government data showed, adding to evidence that the world's number-two economy is heading for a slowdown. (published 0453 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =4TH UPDATE: CHINA PM WEN: AIM FOR GDP GROWTH OF 8.0% IN 2008    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao pledged Wednesday to cap inflation at 4.8% this year and signaled Beijing will continue trying to steer China toward an orderly economic slowdown. (published 0507 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE:AUSTRALIA GDP SLOWS SLIGHTLY BUT DEMAND WHITE HOT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy slowed slightly in the fourth quarter due to sluggish export growth, but domestic demand remained white hot, affirming the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's move to aggressively raise interest rates since mid-2007. (published 0438 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: VIETNAM CTRL BANK: NO CHANGE IN DLR/DONG TRADING BAND    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The  \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Bank of Vietnam\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , hasn't changed the daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading band for the U.S. dollar against the dong from plus or minus 0.75%, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said Wednesday. (published 0439 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PBOC GOV: NO PLAN TO RAISE INTEREST RATE WEDNESDAY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has no plan to raise interest rates Wednesday, People's  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan said. (published 0406 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA BK REGULATOR: CHINA WILL NOT HAVE SUBPRIME CRISIS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China won't have a subprime mortgage crisis like the one in the U.S. that has rocked global markets because it has no zero downpayment mortgages, the nation's top banking regulator said Wednesday. (published 0423 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA FIN MIN: EXPECT ECONOMY TO GROW OVER 8.8% NEXT FY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's economy is expected to grow over 8.8% in the next fiscal year starting April 1, Finance Minister P. Chidambaram said Wednesday. (published 0439 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINE ECON SECY: 1Q GDP UNLIKELY TO RISE 7% ON YEAR    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine economy will unlikely grow 7% on year in the first quarter, failing to sustain the heady pace it achieved in every quarter of 2007, Economic Planning Secretary Augusto Santos said Wednesday. (published 0348 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: PHILIPPINE ON-YEAR INFLATION ACCELERATES IN FEB    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Year-on-year inflation in the Philippines picked up steam in February on higher commodity prices and last year's low base - a development that could prompt the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to suspend further monetary easing. (published 0234 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par KOREA BOND MKT PARTICIPANTS EXPECT BOK RATE NEAR 5% MAR -KSDA    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nearly all bond market participants expect the Bank of Korea to keep its benchmark interest rate target unchanged or close to the current 5.00% this month, according to a survey by the Korea Securities Dealers Association. (published 0210 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: CHINA FIN MIN: TO CUT 2008 BUDGET DEFICIT TO CNY180B    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's government plans to cut its fiscal deficit this year by CNY20 billion, Finance Minister Xie Xuren said Wednesday, reflecting an effort to ease inflationary pressures in the fast-growing economy. (published 0057 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S SWAN: DEMAND NOT MATCHED BY CAPACITY INCREASES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan said gross domestic product figures for the fourth quarter show that strong growth in domestic demand has not been matched by increases in the economy's productive capacity. (published 0150 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA RBA EDEY: HIGHER A$ REFLECTS RISING EXPORT PRICES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia Assistant Governor Malcolm Edey said Wednesday that a higher Australian dollar is reflecting rising export prices. (published 2306 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: TAIWAN TO EASE RULES ON CORP INVESTMENTS IN CHINA    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan will ease rules on corporate investments in China and retroactively approve unauthorized investments by the island's companies there, Premier Chang Chun-hsiung said Wednesday after a weekly meeting of the Cabinet. (published 0530 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINE GOVT:NUMBER OF POOR FILIPINOS +16% IN 2006 VS 2003    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine government Wednesday said the number of poor rose between 2003 and 2006 as salary increases failed to keep up with rising prices of food and basic services. (published 0256 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA FEB PERFORMANCE OF SVC INDEX -1.7 PTS TO 53.2    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's services sector recorded slower growth in February, a performance gauge produced by an industry group published Wednesday shows. (published 2230 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NZ 4Q WHOLESALE TRADE SALES POST RISE; PETROLEUM FUELS GAIN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand wholesale trade sales rose in the fourth quarter of 2007, led by a rise in petroleum sales, official data published Wednesday showed. (published 2217 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S KANGAROO BOND MARKET REMAINS SUPPORTED - RBA    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Kangaroo bond market remains supported by the fundamental factors that have fueled its "robust growth" in recent years, a Reserve Bank of Australia official said Wednesday. (published 0301 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA & NEW ZEALAND BANK OFFERING TOTAL Y135.8B SAMURAI    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia & New Zealand Banking Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  is offering a total of Y135.8 billion of samurai bonds via three tranches, an underwriter said. (published 0128 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UK'S CBI: SERVICE SECTOR OUTLOOK GLOOMIEST SINCE 2001    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tighter credit conditions and flagging consumer demand are darkening the outlook for the U.K.'s dominant service sector, an industry survey said Wednesday. (published 0001 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UK CONSUMER CONFIDENCE HITS FRESH RECORD LOW IN FEBRUARY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. consumer confidence sank to a fresh record low in February as consumers' assessment of current economic conditions weakened sharply, data from U.K. lender Nationwide released Wednesday indicated. (published 0001 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN PM FUKUDA: CAN'T ENVISION VACANT BOJ HEAD SEAT    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Yasuo Fukuda said Wednesday he can't envision a situation where the head of the Bank of Japan's seat is left vacant after the current governor's term expires later this month. (published 0348 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ====== \par  \par =ASIA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :DLR LITTLE CHANGED VS YEN;SENTIMENT STILL BEARISH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was little changed against the yen in Asia on Wednesday as investors stood on the sidelines before the release of U.S. economic data later in the day that may unleash fresh selling of the greenback. (published 0540 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: MALAYSIA JAN EXPORTS LIKELY +6.7% ON YR, IPI +6.2%    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's exports likely improved appreciably in January as higher commodity prices offset weaker electronics shipments, economists said. (published 0514 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =POINT OF VIEW: TWO FED OFFICIALS AND TWO INFLATION VIEWS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The key to the U.S. Federal Reserve's success in helping prop the economy through lower interest rates even as inflation stays higher than desired is to manage people's long-term view of inflation. (published 2116 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW:TAIWAN FEB EXPORT VALUE SEEN LOWEST IN YR ON HOLIDAY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's exports value in February likely fell to a year-low as the Chinese New Year holidays cut the number of working days and because of slower demand from the U.S. and Europe. (published 0239 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MONEY TALKS: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WON'T \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , BARRING UPHEAVAL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar swoon means \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves are again front-page news in Japan, but as long as the yen rise remains orderly, don't expect authorities to resort to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. (published 2345 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VIEW: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO PROP DOLLAR VS EURO UNLIKELY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European officials can jawbone all they want about the euro's sharp rise against the dollar, but until U.S. officials start agreeing with them, the probability of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains low. (published 2026 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJA(3/5) DOLLAR'S TUMBLE PINCHES JAPAN'S EXPORTERS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's biggest exporters are feeling the pressure of the weaker dollar, raising the prospects that their earnings might finally take a big hit in coming quarters after years of solid growth. (published 2131 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/5) NO ANSWERS YET TO A TRILLION-DOLLAR QUESTION    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turmoil continues in the municipal-bond market. This week tax-free municipals have been yielding more than taxable Treasurys, a rare anomaly. Auctions of variable-rate municipals have been faltering. (published 0056 GMT)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =IN THE MONEY: SOVEREIGN FUNDS LET $30B RIDE ON CITI, MERRILL    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sovereign wealth funds that recently plowed billions of dollars into major U.S. financial institutions so far are living anything but the "American Dream." (published 2107 GMT) [ 05-03-08 0600GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080305e4350007t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
