\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Yen surge creating vicious circle for Japan   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs   \par \uc2 910 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2008\par 08:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 28 (Reuters) - The yen's surge to a three-year high against the dollar threatens to trap an already tepid economy in a vicious circle, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp gains hitting local stocks and undermining the country's key exporters.   \par \par \uc2 A strong yen is making life tricky for Japanese policymakers because it has almost nothing to do with the economy's own health, instead reflecting the fear gripping investors around the world about a painful U.S. recession.   \par \par \uc2 That fear and a flare up in market volatility prompted investors and speculators to unwind carry trades -- borrowing in yen at Japan's meagre 0.5 percent short-term rates to buy high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , emerging markets stocks or commodities.   \par \par \uc2 The yen's role as a funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of riskier assets, in turn, has tied its fortunes to the performance of stock markets in Japan and abroad, even as expectations mount that the Bank of Japan may need to trim rates this year to help the economy.   \par \par \uc2 "The Japan-specific factors would normally work to soften the yen, not strengthen it. This is the unfortunate result," said Tomoko Fujii, senior economist and strategist at  \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 MIRROR IMAGE   \par \par \uc2 Economists at  \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  said on Monday that Japan may have already fallen into a recession.   \par \par \uc2 The yen's sensitivity to stocks was highlighted on Monday when a 4 percent slide in the Nikkei share average <.N225> boosted the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and dragged the dollar down to near 106.00 yen <JPY=>.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar touched a low of 104.95 yen last week, its weakest since May 2005, after the Federal Reserve's 75 basis point emergency rate cut last Tuesday spurred a brief recovery in stocks.   \par \par \uc2 One reflection of the yen's tight links to equity markets is that yen's day-to-day moves in the past year have been almost a mirror image of the Nikkei.   \par \par \uc2 In 2007 the yen climbed against the dollar 92 percent of the time the Nikkei fell, according to Reuters data. The year before, that correlation was just 28 percent.   \par \par \uc2 If stocks sell off further, it could drive the yen to heights that will start to test the pain threshold of Japan's exporters, such as Toyota or  \uc2 Sony\uc2  , one of the mainstays of the world's second-biggest economy.   \par \par \uc2 The selling of exporter shares is why the Nikkei share average has been hit even harder than U.S. or European stocks, despite the limited exposure of Japanese banks and investors to the U.S. subprime mortgage crisis.   \par \par \uc2 On Monday the dollar was down as much as 4.7 percent against the yen since the start of the year, on track for its biggest one-month drop since August 2001, potentially catching off guard exporters who had planned for a higher level.   \par \par \uc2 A Reuters survey of Japan's manufacturers this month found they expected a rate near 108.50 to the dollar at the end of February.   \par \par \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2  Chief Executive Howard Stringer told Reuters at the World Economic Forum over the weekend that the yen is volatile and "we watch it with some anxiety".   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  SPECULATION   \par \par \uc2 Such a spike in the yen has inevitably stirred speculation that Japanese authorities may resort to dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, four years after Tokyo spent 15 trillion yen ($140 billion) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter of 2004.   \par \par \uc2 But analysts say that the circumstances have changed when it comes to the politics of a rising yen that will make it difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and almost impossible on such a scale.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , along with other wealthy nations making up the Group of Seven, has prodded China to unshackle its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Beijing's tight day-to-day management via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hosting of the Group's meetings this year, including that of G7 finance ministers next month in Tokyo, and would find itself in an awkward situation resuming \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  while pushing Beijing to loosen its grip over the yuan.   \par \par \uc2 Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Japan at  \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 , said in a note to clients last week that the G7 pressure on China and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's own towering debt levels make a large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  difficult to pull off.   \par \par \uc2 Sasaki, who once conducted the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would do so on a small scale.   \par \par \uc2 Even if the yen breaks through 100 to the dollar for the first time since 1995, Sasaki and other analysts believe the craving of Japanese investors for higher-yielding foreign assets will prevent any further gains.   \par \par \uc2 The insatiable hunger of Japanese investors for foreign assets, along with the popularity of carry trades, drove the yen's real trade-weighted value to a 22-year low last July.   \par \par \uc2 "Their appetite for foreign assets will not disappear because of the fall in U.S. interest rates. There is no hope for Japanese interest rates to rise," said Masafumi Yamamoto, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for Japan at  \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 , adding that household investors are in a wait-and-see mode for now. (Editing by Tomasz Janowski) ($1=106.68 Yen)   \par \par \uc2 YEN/ (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|Z|UKI|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080128e41s000km\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
