\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Nukaga: Want G7 To Discuss Policy Coordination   \b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2008\par 05:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more Nukaga comments, BOJ Gov. Fukui's remarks and background.)   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga said Monday he wants to find areas where the Group of Seven industrialized countries can work together to deal with recent global market turbulence at the group's meeting next week.   \par \par \uc2 "As chairman of the meeting, I want to discuss where policy coordination is possible among the G7 members (in response to recent instability global financial markets) at the meeting, and I want to issue a message," Nukaga told a budget committee at the Lower House of Parliament.   \par \par \uc2 But he said each G7 member must first understand its own situation and take appropriate policy responses.   \par \par \uc2 "Before coordinating policy, each country must first grasp its own financial markets' and economy's situations and take steps. Building on these steps, we'd like to find out where coordination is possible among the G7," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Nukaga's qualified remarks on policy coordination suggest it may be hard for the G7 nations to cobble together a concerted plan of action for dealing with the market jitters because of differences in the state of their economies.   \par \par \uc2 The Japanese finance minister also said he wants to hear more at the meeting about the U.S. subprime loan issue and how it is affecting the U.S. economy.   \par \par \uc2 "The current falling global stock prices and financial market instability stem from the U.S. subprime issue. I'd like to know how the problem is being addressed by the U.S. at the (G7) meeting."   \par \par \uc2 At the same Lower House committee session, BOJ Gov. Toshihiko Fukui said the recent falls in Japanese stock prices are partly the result of a stronger yen.   \par \par \uc2 "Investors' proclivity to take risks has receded" amid uncertainties over the global economic outlook, and "that's basically a common factor that has driven Japanese stocks lower," along with share markets in other countries, Fukui said. But "there's a possibility that the yen's strength may (also) have been another factor causing stock prices to fall," Fukui added.   \par \par \uc2 Fukui has made similar remarks several times last week.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players interpret those remarks as a general description of market conditions, rather than as a signal \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is preparing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to hold down the yen.   \par \par \uc2 In a recent interview with Dow Jones Newswires, former senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Eisuke Sakakibara expressed a similar view, saying the MOF is unlikely to step in.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't think (the ministry) will be able to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "At a time when the U.S. (and other advanced economies) are pressing China to revalue the yuan by a big margin, it would look awkward if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Sakakibara said.   \par \par \uc2 The country's benchmark Nikkei 225 Stock Average ended the morning session down 2.6% at 13274.93. At 0245GMT, the dollar was changing hands at Y106.70, little changed from Friday's New York closing.   \par \par \uc2 The meeting of financial heads of the G7 countries will be held Feb. 9 in Tokyo. As the finance minister of the host country, Nukaga will chair the meeting.   \par \par \uc2 -By Takeshi Takeuchi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 (Takashi Nakamichi contributed to this item.) [ 28-01-08 0425GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080128e41s0004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
