\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW:Ex-MOF Sakakibara Sees '08 Japan Growth 1% Or Less   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2008\par 03:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's growth rate could fall to 1.0% or less this year, even if the Bank of Japan brings interest rates back to zero, said a former top Japanese finance ministry official.   \par \par \uc2 Problems ranging from decelerating global growth, to fragile Tokyo stocks, to a rising cost of living at home could put the brakes on Japan's economy, which economists estimate expanded by a little less than 2.0% last year, said former Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar could also, in the coming months, sink to as low as Y95, its lowest point against the yen since 1995, Sakakibara said. That's 11% lower than the Y106.75 at which the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded in Asia early Monday. A strong yen makes Japanese exports less competitive in global markets - not a good thing for an economy that relies on overseas demand for much of its growth.   \par \par \uc2 "Japan's growth rate could be below 1.0%" in 2008, Sakakibara said in a recent interview with Dow Jones Newswires. "The chance of a recession (in Japan) also is not zero."   \par \par \uc2 For the fiscal year starting in April, the economy may grow only around 1.0%, he said, calling the government's official target for an inflation-adjusted 2.0% expansion for fiscal 2008 "too optimistic."   \par \par \uc2 Slowing growth in the U.S. and around the world are dragging down the exports that have propelled Japanese growth over recent quarters, threatening corporate earnings and hurting business sentiment. Confidence among Japanese consumers is also down, as wages barely budge while costlier commodities push up prices for everything from gasoline to food to air travel.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Zero Interest, Zero Impact \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nervous Japanese politicians, including Bank Minister Yoshimi Watanabe and his predecessor Yuji Yamamoto, have called for the Bank of Japan to lower interest rates to stimulate growth. But that's a misguided approach, Sakakibara said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ's current target for overnight loans of 0.5% is "sufficiently accommodative," said Sakakibara. "Even if the Bank of Japan does lower rates ... a cut of 0.5 percentage point will be too little to have any great impact. I wouldn't recommend such a policy step."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At a news conference last week, BOJ Gov. Toshihiko Fukui dropped hints the bank wouldn't rule out a rate cut if the economy deteriorates, though he later indicated he wouldn't rush it.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    MOF Won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Even If Dollar Falls \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's grim outlook, the dollar could fall sharply versus the yen because U.S. economic prospects are even gloomier, Sakakibara said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The U.S. economy is no doubt decelerating," he said. "If the degree of the slowdown is serious, the dollar will decline against the yen."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the U.S. has loosened both fiscal and monetary policies to spur growth, "the economy there has yet to recover fully from the subprime (mortgage market) problems, so there are very strong concerns about a potential recession," he said. Odds of a U.S. recession, or two straight quarters of contraction, this year are "50% or higher," he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sakakibara also said debates about the U.S. and global economies are likely to dominate the meeting scheduled for Feb. 9 of officials from the Group of Seven leading industrialized countries.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , meanwhile, is unlikely to try to break the greenback's fall, Sakakibara said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think (the ministry) will be able to conduct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Sakakibara, whose ability to influence the dollar-yen rate during his Finance Ministry career earned him the nickname "Mr. Yen." That's because the U.S. government, from which Tokyo must get permission before nudging the dollar higher or lower, seems loath to see \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Sakakibara said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "At a time when the U.S. (and other advanced economies) are pressing China to revalue the yuan by a big margin, it would look awkward if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he said. "Telling China to revalue is like telling it to abandon \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Against that background, I don't think \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Not Interested In BOJ Governor's Post   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commenting on the political debate over who should replace BOJ Gov. Fukui when he steps down in March, Sakakibara said he, at least, isn't interested in the job.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What would I be able to do as BOJ governor?" said Sakakibara, currently a professor at Waseda University in Tokyo. "That would be just to raise or lower interest rates. I don't find such a job attractive. There are many other things I want to do."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc