\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 :Dlr Swings Wildly Vs Yen; May Fall Below Y105   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Mochizuki   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2008\par 07:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar swung wildly against the yen in Asia Tuesday, sinking to a 32-month-low before jumping up by a yen, as short-term focused players sought to make profits in both directions of trade amid volatile market moves.   \par \par \uc2 While a number of Tokyo dealers anticipate violent price movements may last for the coming week, they also expect the dollar to eventually fall below Y105.   \par \par \uc2 "Many dealers are expecting the yen to strengthen," said Jun Kato, senior manager at  \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\uc2  "The yen isn't an attractive unit to buy, but it is the least unpopular \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  among the dollar, euro and yen in the current market turmoil."   \par \par \uc2 Societe Generale's head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Yuji Saito shared a similar view, saying "the dollar will resume its drop later in the day as Dow (Jones Industrial Average) futures are falling by 450 points, suggesting New York stock prices will drop later."   \par \par \uc2 Earlier Tuesday, short-term investors sent the dollar down to Y105.61, a level unseen since May 2005, speculating that Japan's Nikkei 225 Stock Average would plunge. But as they failed to push the dollar down below support at Y105.50, they bought back the dollar to take profits. The rise was partly due to a vague rumor that the Federal Reserve will soon hold an unscheduled meeting and cut its federal funds rate by 75 basis point.   \par \par \uc2 "Today's Tokyo market is almost dominated by orders from super-short-term players," said Takuma Kurosawa, treasurer at  \uc2 HSBC.\par \par \uc2 In a reflection of how the market expects \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves to remain sharp, dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  option prices jumped, with one-month volatilities spiking to 16.5%/16.7% from 13.5%/14% Monday. Some traders said it might soon climb above 20%, the highest level since August 2007, indicating that the option market thinks the dollar may fall sharply against the yen in coming weeks.   \par \par \uc2 Most dealers consider the rumor to be position talk and don't expect any rate-cuts before the next Federal Open Market Committee between Jan. 29-30. But they said the rumor reflects the market's view on the Fed's policy direction.   \par \par \uc2 "The rumor means that players have priced in, or are even demanding, the Fed's 75-basis-point rate cut next week," said Masashi Matsuzawa, senior dealer at  \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank.\uc2  "Even if the FOMC cuts its rates by that much, that wouldn't drastically ease the market's confusion. It needs to go beyond the market's expectation."   \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga's comment overnight on the dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mostly fell in line with the market expectations and was received rather calmly by traders.   \par \par \uc2 In an interview overnight with Dow Jones Newswires, Nukaga said "we are not thinking about" carrying out a large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the one Tokyo once did between 2003 and 2004. At the time, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  totaled Y35 trillion. The finance minister repeated the comment at a regular press conference Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 "We have already almost ruled out the chance of an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,"  \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Kato said.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Bank of Japan, as expected, left its policy rate untouched at 0.50%. Players will be paying attention to Gov. Toshihiko Fukui's press conference later in the day to see whether he says something optimistic about the world economy. But since players are focused on global stock price movements and the Federal Reserve's next moves, Fukui's comments are unlikely to influence market actions much, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 As of 0450 GMT, the dollar stood at Y106.01 from Y106.06 in Canada overnight. The euro was at $1.4411 from $1.4441 and Y152.79 from Y153.13.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 23:50 EST / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              106.01-05  105.93-96  +0.08  106.50  105.63   -4.88 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.4411-14  1.4446-47  -0.24  1.4459  1.4392   -1.28 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.9378-82  1.9430-33  -0.27  1.9441  1.9375   -2.48 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.1108-10  1.1085-87  +0.21  1.1121  1.1082   -1.97 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     1.033-35   1.0347-51  -0.16  1.0358  1.0316   +2.65 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   0.8565-69  0.8608-10  -0.50  0.8625  0.8563   -2.28 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.7422-32  0.7447-51  -0.34  0.7471  0.7402   -3.14 \par  \par Euro Rates \par  \par EUR/JPY Yen              152.78-83  153.03-07  -0.16  153.83  152.31   -6.00    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2935; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com [ 22-01-08 0606GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1034 1863 28038 4162   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080122e41m000a3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
