\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: Has Japan Gone To Ground On The Yen?   \b0\par\par\uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2008\par 00:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Rosalind Mathieson   \par \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The continued rise in the Japanese yen, despite weak stock markets there and an economy which does not justify or need a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will be causing some closed-door conniptions in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's hands are somewhat tied on the matter, but that doesn't mean it has no options available to it to try and keep a lid on the yen.   \par \par \uc2 As this column has noted before, expect this behind-the-scenes concern to spill into the public arena if the yen continues to defy fundamentals.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been fairly quiet on the matter so far (as have other officials).   \par \par \uc2 But while the U.S. dollar would probably have to fall further against the yen to risk forcing the MOF's hand (say 100 or below against the yen), it would be naive to think it has gone to ground - or that it is okay with the current trend in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2 The yen has been benefiting from risk aversion for some time, as investors unwind carry trades and as some go outright long the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 That's despite the Nikkei 225 being just as caught up in global share market weakness as other bourses in Asia.   \par \par \uc2 And that's despite an economy in Japan which is barely crawling along, and interest rates which remain at rock-bottom prices.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar in January has fallen from around 111.61 against the yen to around 106.50 now. That's within spitting distance of its 2.5-year low of 105.92.   \par \par \uc2 The euro has been similarly hit against the yen, as have the higher-yielding names like the Australian and New Zealand dollars, and the British pound.   \par \par \uc2 That has to be causing some angst in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 Outright \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a low risk, for a variety of reasons.   \par \par \uc2 The biggest one is Japan would be unlikely to get the support of the Americans before it went into the market - and it wouldn't want to act without U.S. approval.   \par \par \uc2 Japan has also been one of the many nations which have singled out China for keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially weak, and it wouldn't want the loss of face by then being seen to do the same thing.   \par \par \uc2 It is hosting a G7 finance ministers meeting in early February. Imagine the timing - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and then has a host of G7 ministers land on its doorstep. Ouch.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's "Mr Yen" - former vice finance minister for international affairs Eisuke Sakakibara - who still pops up frequently to proffer his views on the market, says that even if the dollar falls to around 102, government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is highly unlikely.   \par \par \uc2 But don't rule out verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It wouldn't have to be strongly worded - really just a tweak in the current rhetoric - to have some effect. Especially if all the officials were taking the same line, showing the broader strategy in place.   \par \par \uc2 For those thinking the yen can continue to surf the risk aversion wave higher indefinitely, it would also pay to remember the rocks that lie beneath.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan is very unlikely to be raising interest rates anytime soon. Even if U.S. rates come down more, and countries like Australia stop hiking rates, Japan rates are hardly competitive.   \par \par \uc2 A sharp U.S. economic downturn, especially if it becomes a recession, would hit the Japanese economy hard, especially if its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were relatively high at the time.   \par \par \uc2 That may do some of the work for Japanese officials, in terms of taking the heat out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 But they may not want to wait for fundamentals to do their part. If the yen keeps rising in coming weeks, the pressure to respond via verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become too large to ignore.   \par \par \uc2 -By Rosalind Mathieson, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4141, rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 (Rosalind Mathieson is the Managing Editor for Dow Jones Markets News in Asia. She has 13 years of experience as a political and financial reporter and editor, having worked previously at Australian Associated Press in Sydney and Canberra.)   \par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 17-01-08 2347GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1171 70779   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080117e41h00159\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
