\par\par \b \uc2 Focus: Traders Scent BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  As Yen Keeps Climbing   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Martin   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2008\par 17:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The yen's appreciation against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prompting speculation about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the country's exporters.   \par \par \uc2 However, analysts caution that the yen would have to make significant further gains before the BoJ would contemplate an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given both the need to be very sure the move would reverse the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's direction, and given the likely hostility it would provoke among other members of the Group of Seven leading developed economies.   \par \par \uc2 The trigger for a fresh yen climb in European hours Tuesday came from tumbling Japanese equities. The benchmark Nikkei index closed below 14,000 for the first time since November 2005 as investors mulled poor signals on the Japanese economy.   \par \par \uc2 When foreign investors buy Japanese equities, they usually sell yen to take the sting out of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, which makes equities and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move in opposite directions.   \par \par \uc2 With these investors now shunning Japanese equities, there's less yen selling, pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. And as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength generally hurts Japanese exporters, the cycle has become tough to break.   \par \par \uc2 But pressure on exporters is the last thing Japan's economic policy makers want to see, with consumer spending yet to stage a sustained recovery and a renewed bout of deflation still a threat.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's attention has therefore turned to the prospects for an official Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, with the Y101 level against the dollar seen as a key potential point of entry.   \par \par \uc2 But Sebastien Galy, a strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort in London, warned that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be further away even than that.   \par \par \uc2 "If they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they won't want to screw it up," he said. "When they do come in, they will want to do it strongly and effectively, and they're not even saying anything about it yet."   \par \par \uc2 Galy's best bet is that the Japanese authorities will resist the urge to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at previously favored levels around Y100 to Y101 against the dollar, instead waiting until the yen climbs to the Y98 area.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen in March 2004, and other G7 members tolerated the move given the threat that deflation posed to the world's second largest economy. Since then, however, G7 tolerance of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  has diminished. Indeed, the group's argument that China is manipulating its yuan would be weakened if one of its own were to do the same.   \par \par \uc2 Japan holds the G7 presidency this year, and hosts a meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the group on February 9. It would likely seek their assent before embarking on an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and do so without the approval of other G7 members only if the risks it perceived to economic growth were very serious.   \par \par \uc2 The yen's gains are being fueled in part by a reluctance to hold dollars given the likelihood that bad economic news will prompt the U.S. Federal Reserve to cut its key interest rate again.   \par \par \uc2 "Over the short term, there's a lot of bad news out there, and there's a lot of potential bad news from the US data coming up over the course of this week," said Colin Asher, an analyst at Nomura in London. "That means there's a reluctance to hold dollars at any price, rather than a bullish view on the yen."   \par \par \uc2 The result Tuesday was that the dollar slipped to Y107.34 in early European hours - matching levels last seen in November last year. And the euro sank to Y159.93 - also a November low.   \par \par \uc2 Grim US retail sales data in New York hours later Tuesday hit the dollar even harder, dropping to Y106.61 - a two-and-a-half year low. Euro/yen also tumbled to Y158.87.   \par \par \uc2 Technical analysts now say that if the dollar maintains its break below Y107.20, there could be a rapid plunge ahead. Nicole Elliott, a technical strategist at Mizuho in London, said the dollar could easily drop to the Y105 area, while a break below Y159.75 for the euro could spark an initial move down to Y158, followed by an even steeper dip.   \par \par \uc2 It may be too soon to get overly bearish on euro/yen, particularly with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  clearly so reluctant to cut interest rates. But this week's schedule of US economic data, combined with a set of speakers from the US Federal Reserve, leaves the dollar potentially primed for a broad-based fall.   \par \par \uc2 For euro/yen, Galy points out that last year's low was Y150, so the yen has a lot of room left to climb before warning bells start ringing.   \par \par \uc2 And as the move higher so far has been smooth and gradual rather than jerky and disorderly, there's little scope to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a good case can be made that the stronger yen is really hurting the economy.   \par \par \uc2 At 1530 GMT, the dollar was at Y106.78 while the euro was at Y159.00.   \par \par \uc2 -By Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 (0) 207 842 9346; katie.martin@dowjones.com [ 01-15-08 1105ET ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCS000020080115e41f000al\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
