\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Japan's 2008/09 budget   \b0\par\par\uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 2007\par 05:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Click on [ID:nT201213] for a story on the budget, on [ID:nTKU002933] for a story on JGB issuance)   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 24 (Reuters) - Following are highlights of the Japanese government's planned budget for fiscal 2008/09 starting in April, based on figures provided by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  BUDGET SIZE   \par \par \uc2  -- The size of the government's budget for fiscal 2008/09 that starts in April will be around 83.06 trillion yen, according to the final draft, up 0.2 percent from this year's initial budget of 82.91 trillion yen.   \par \par \uc2  -- Of the total, the government will use 47.28 trillion yen for general expenditure. The figure, covering everything from defence and public works to social welfare and education, is up 0.7 percent from 46.98 trillion yen this year and rises for a second consecutive year.  -- The government will transfer 15.61 trillion yen to municipal governments as tax grants, up 4.6 percent from 14.93 trillion yen in fiscal 2007/08.   \par \par \uc2  -- The remainder, 20.16 trillion yen, will be used to service debt. Debt servicing costs for the current year were put at 21 trillion yen in the initial budget.   \par \par \uc2  -- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's estimate of debt service costs is based on an assumed 10-year Japanese government bond yield of 2.0 percent.   \par \par \uc2  -- Tax revenues are estimated at 53.55 trillion yen, up from this year's initial budget forecast of 53.47 trillion yen.   \par \par \uc2     However, this year's figure is expected to be revised down to 52.55 trillion yen as tax revenue fell short of the initial estimate due to a fall in corporate and income tax revenue because of slower-than-expected economic growth.   \par \par \uc2  -- Official development assistance (ODA) for poor countries is set to be cut by 4.0 percent from this year's budget to 700.2 billion yen.   \par \par \uc2  -- Defence-related spending is cut by 0.5 percent to 4.78 trillion yen.   \par \par \uc2  -- Spending on social welfare will rise 3.0 percent to 21.78 trillion yen as the ageing population requires more spending on pensions and medical services.   \par \par \uc2  -- While the government is trying to trim spending in most areas, spending on steps to help small firms will increase 7.3 percent from this year's initial budget to 176.1 billion yen.   \par \par \uc2  -- Spending on public works will be reduced 3.1 percent to 6.74 trillion yen.   \par \par \uc2  BOND ISSUANCE   \par \par \uc2  -- Fresh issuance of Japanese government bonds (JGBs) is planned at 25.35 trillion yen, down from 25.43 trillion yen in this year's initial budget. The amount of new debt issuance is the lowest since fiscal 1997/98 when it was 18.5 trillion yen.   \par \par \uc2  -- Japan will rely on bonds for 30.5 percent of its overall budget in fiscal 2008/09, slightly up from a revised 30.3 percent this year.   \par \par \uc2  -- Japan's outstanding public debt, including that held by central and local governments, is expected to rise to around 776 trillion yen at the end of next fiscal 2008/09, roughly 147 percent of the gross domestic product.   \par \par \uc2  -- The government plans to use 9.8 trillion yen from reserves in a special account for "zaito" fiscal investment and loan programmes to redeem outstanding debt.   \par \par \uc2  FOREIGN \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  ACCOUNT   \par \par \uc2  -- A special account that holds \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets acquired through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , called the \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund Special Account (FEFSA), will continue to transfer surplus funds to the general account.   \par \par \uc2  -- For next fiscal year, 1.8 trillion yen will be transferred from the FEFSA to help support the general account budget.   \par \par \uc2  FY 2007/08 EXTRA BUDGET   \par \par \uc2  -- The government is to adopt a 1.78 trillion yen extra budget for the current fiscal year to next March, partly to postpone a rise in medical charges for aged patients and for a package of measures including help for small firms with fuel costs.   \par \par \uc2  FILP   \par \par \uc2  -- The Fiscal Investment and Loan Programme (FILP), under which the government offers funds to a wide range of semi-governmental corporations and banks, for fiscal 2008/09 is projected at 13.87 trillion yen, down 2.1 percent from an initial projection of 14.16 trillion yen this fiscal year.   \par \par \uc2  -- The programme has been shrinking since 2000 as a part of public sector reforms.  ($1=114.11 Yen)  (Reporting by Yuzo Saeki; Editing by Alan Raybould)     \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BUDGET (FACTBOX)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|J|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071224e3co0005b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
