\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-On charts, the dollar's slide targets 105 yen   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs   \par \uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2007\par 07:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 5 (Reuters) - The dollar's bounce from a 2-1/2-year low against the yen last week gives the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least some temporary respite on the charts, but a fall towards 105 yen and even 100 seems probable next year.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar's jump to a high of 111.23 last Friday created what's called a bullish engulfing pattern on a weekly basis that helps provide a base of support, giving it scope to climb towards 112.00 in the near term.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar narrowly avoided a more negative technical shadow by managing to hold above 110 yen at November's monthly close, which would have been the first monthly close below that level since May 2005.   \par \par \uc2 But technical analysts say a further slide is likely given the violent break of its steady uptrend since 2005 and the fact that some other pairs, especially sterling/yen, have formed clear patterns suggesting a medium-term drop is in store.   \par \par \uc2 A dollar rise above 115 yen is needed to break the downtrend and make a push towards this year's peak of 124.14 seen in June, analysts say, but such a shift is unlikely to happen.   \par \par \uc2 "Given this big downturn in the dollar is due to concerns over the U.S. subprime mortgage crisis and credit-related losses at financial institutions, the dollar is highly unlikely to rise above that level and reverse its current trend," said Kengo Suzuki, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Shinko Securities.   \par \par \uc2 Suzuki said there was a 95 percent chance the dollar could fall towards 101.50 yen next year -- a level near which the Japanese authorities have repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past to prevent any yen appreciation back into double-digit territory.   \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday the dollar changed hands near 110.30 yen <JPY=>, holding off a 2-1/2-year low of 107.22 struck last week.   \par \par \uc2 105 ON THE HORIZON   \par \par \uc2 The 105 yen level is key because it marks an important zone where the dollar has bottomed in the past few years, especially in late 2003 and 2004, when the Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively to prevent yen gains towards the 100 line.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent about $140 billion buying dollars in the first quarter of 2004 alone, when the dollar fell as low as 103.40 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Looking back over the past decade or so, the two major levels for the dollar remain the record low of 79.70 yen in 1995, from which it soared all the way up to 147.60 in 1998.   \par \par \uc2 Since that 1998 peak -- when the Russian debt default and near-collapse of hedge fund giant LTCM sparked a massive unwinding of carry trades -- the dollar has not fallen under 101 yen. The lowest it has got was near 101.25 yen in late 1999.   \par \par \uc2 A fall through 105 would target those 101 lows and possibly pave the way for a tumble back into double-digit territory for the first time since 1995.   \par \par \uc2 Nicole Elliott, a technical strategist at  \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in London, favours a fall towards 105 yen and notes that the dollar could be about to experience the rarity of back-to-back quarterly falls, depending on how December plays out.   \par \par \uc2 Elliott reckoned the performance of Asian and some European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen suggested the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready for a bigger rise, saying sterling/yen's <GBPJPY=R> large head-and-shoulders this year indicated a "major topping pattern".   \par \par \uc2 Other chart-watchers have said dollar/yen is converging within a broad sideways triangle pattern between the 1995 low and 1998 high.   \par \par \uc2 How that triangle is broken will determine the medium-term direction.   \par \par \uc2 Last week's 107.22 low took the dollar right near the bottom end of that triangle -- the upward trendline from the 1995 low that also touches the January 2005 trough.   \par \par \uc2 The upper end of that triangle drawn off the 1998 high and this year's peak is now near 122.60.   \par \par \uc2 David Solin, a partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, said there was a risk of the dollar trading back towards the top of that triangle. The next few weeks would be a period of consolidation to determine the importance of the 107/108 low, he said in a note to clients.   \par \par \uc2 Suzuki at Shinko Securities also says 108 is important when looking at a histogram that gauges how often the dollar finished within one yen intervals at the New York close between January 2000 and early November this year.   \par \par \uc2 "The histogram, too, tells the 108 yen level is a psychologically important level for the dollar," Suzuki said. (Additional reporting by Rika Otsuka; Editing by Alan Raybould)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-YEN/ (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|U|NAT|J|ABX|BNX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071208e3c50006s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
