\par\par \b \uc2 Japan lends  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserve bonds to aid liquidity -MOF   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa   \par \uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2007\par 06:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 22 (Reuters) - Japan has been lending securities from its foreign reserves as a way to provide liquidity to bond markets overseas and more effectively manage its reserves, the world's second-largest after those of China.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's foreign reserves stood at a record $954.5 billion at the end of October, most of which were held as securities such as bonds, and markets are keen to know what Tokyo does with this massive stockpile.   \par \par \uc2 "Lending securities helps provide liquidity to markets and it also brings us income," a finance ministry official told Reuters.   \par \par \uc2 In its first detailed disclosure of income gains from managing its external reserves, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said this month that it earned 8.459 billion yen ($78 million) from foreign security lending in the fiscal year that ended on March 31.   \par \par \uc2 The balance of its lending stood at $69.596 billion as of the end of October, including similar security lending by the Bank of Japan. It started lending out such securities about two years ago.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan held $46.2 billion in foreign assets as of the end of March this year, which is counted as part of Japan's overall foreign reserves. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started its own foreign security lending scheme in 2003, and its lending balance stood at 1.576 trillion yen ($14.52 billion) at the end of March.   \par \par \uc2 The MOF, which is in charge of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, does not give \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdowns of its external reserves.   \par \par \uc2 But historical data on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has mostly taken the form of dollar buying, suggests most of Tokyo's hefty reserves are in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 In recent years, the MOF has been diversifying the range of dollar-denominated assets in its foreign reserves partly to avoid an excessive accumulation of U.S. Treasuries.   \par \par \uc2 It has also been buying and selling securities in the secondary market, using private brokerages, rather than simply buying new issues and holding them to maturity.   \par \par \uc2 But the ministry remains cautious about more aggressive ways of managing its reserves, such as the idea of setting up a new investment body to seek bigger profits, stressing that it wants to ensure safety and liquidity before profitability.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo does not hold stocks or real estate in its reserves and has no plan to divert its reserves out of dollars into euros, saying a top priority is to avoid distorting the market.   \par \par \uc2 The MOF decided to issue the details of income gains on the reserves this month to improve the transparency of its reserves management.   \par \par \uc2 Its data showed that income gains from the reserves amounted to 3.692 trillion yen ($34 billion) in fiscal 2006/07, which ended on March 31, with interest on investment assets averaging 4.0 percent.   \par \par \uc2 If both income and capital gains are included, interest on investment assets averaged roughly 6.7 percent in 2006/07.   \par \par \uc2 Interest rate income from term deposits held in reserves stood at 648.8 billion yen in fiscal 2006/07, while that from securities amounted to 2.627 trillion yen.   \par \par \uc2 -- To view the MOF's guidelines for the management of foreign reserves, see the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  Web site:   \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mof.go.jp/english/if/050404.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mof.go.jp/english/if/050404.htm  }}}\uc2  (Editing by Hugh Lawson)   \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/RESERVES|LANGEN|J|ABN|M|E|RBN|U|D|NAT|ABX|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071122e3bm0009l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
