\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan distances itself from sovereign fund idea   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa and Tamawa Kadoya   \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2007\par 08:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Add more comments, background, byline)   \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Oct 20 (Reuters) - The way Japan manages its foreign reserves should have a neutral impact on financial markets so it is not desirable to manage them aggressively, a Japanese official said late on Friday, playing down the idea of creating a sovereign wealth fund.   \par \par \uc2 The senior finance ministry official said Japan seeks liquidity and safety in managing the reserves and would only consider profitability after those factors had been taken into consideration -- a mantra of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in charge of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy .   \par \par \uc2 Some academics and analysts say Japan's foreign reserves are now too big and it should consider managing part of the stockpile in a more effective and aggressive manner.   \par \par \uc2 "For a country like Japan, which manages its economy based on market mechanisms, it is not desirable for the government to manage foreign assets and invest in various ways aggressively," the official said, when asked whether Japan should set up a sovereign wealth fund.   \par \par \uc2 "It is important that our reserve management has a neutral impact on markets," the official told reporters.   \par \par \uc2 He was speaking after a meeting of the finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the Group of Seven rich nations in Washington.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's official reserves, the world's second largest after China's, rose to just over $945 billion in September from $932 billion in August, figures earlier in October showed.   \par \par \uc2 REACHING OUT   \par \par \uc2 The G7 held an "outreach" dinner with countries that operate special investment funds, called "sovereign wealth funds" (SWFs), including China, South Korea, Kuwait, Norway, Russia, Saudi Arabia, Singapore and the United Arab Emirates.   \par \par \uc2 The Japanese official said the dinner was not meant for the G7 to tell those countries what to do.   \par \par \uc2 "It was not a meeting to invite those countries with SWFs and get them angry," he told reporters.   \par \par \uc2 Rather, the G7 members said that SWFs can contribute to stability in markets through relatively long-term investments, unlike hedge funds that heavily use leverage, he said.   \par \par \uc2 At the same time, the session pointed to concerns that those funds could affect markets by acting differently from private funds. Worries about national security were also raised, the official said.   \par \par \uc2 But G7 sources said there were no discussions about trying to impose any particular curbs or disclosure requirements on sovereign wealth funds.   \par \par \uc2 "There didn't seem to be any point to the meeting," a G7 source told Reuters, adding that the countries with large sovereign wealth funds that attended the meeting merely explained their investments and the G7 welcomed inward investment.   \par \par \uc2 CALLS ON JAPAN   \par \par \uc2 Japan's foreign reserves have accumulated from years of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004 but its reserves have been gradually growing on interest rate income from foreign bonds and deposits in reserve holdings.   \par \par \uc2 Takatoshi Ito, a University of Tokyo professor who is one of four private-sector members of the government's top economic council, has said the government should set up a fund to manage the interest rate income so that it helps avoid the need for extra issuance of public debt.   \par \par \uc2 Currently, the government has to issue short-term government debt to balance a special budget account that holds Japan's foreign reserves when the asset side of the balance sheet increases on interest rate income from deposits and securities held in the reserves.   \par \par \uc2 But the ministry has been cautious about creating a new public agency to more actively manage foreign reserves. It has also repeated many times that it has no plan to diversify the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  make-up of its reserves.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdown of Japan's external reserves is a state secret, but historical data on the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests that most of its reserves are in dollars.   \par \par \uc2 G7-JAPAN/RESERVES (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071020e3ak00086\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
