\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan foreign reserves $945.601 bln at end-Sept   \b0\par\par\uc2 333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2007\par 04:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, background)   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 5 (Reuters) - Japan's foreign reserves, the world's second largest, rose to a record $945.601 billion at the end of September, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Lower U.S. interest rates inflated the value of U.S. bonds held in the reserves, while the euro's rise against the dollar also boosted the value of euro denominated bonds, a finance ministry official said.   \par \par \uc2 The reserves were up from $932.157 billion at the end of August, rising for the fourth straight month and marking a record high for the third month in a row.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves, second only to those of China, ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounting to a record 20 trillion yen ($175 billion) in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004, as Tokyo tried to keep a rapid rise in the yen from derailing a then-fragile economic recovery and accelerating deflation.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has kept out of the market since then, but the reserves have continued to grow slowly partly on interest rate income.   \par \par \uc2 The government does not disclose the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdown of the external reserves. But historical data on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has mostly taken the form of dollar buying, suggests most of Tokyo's hefty reserves are in dollars.   \par \par \uc2 Japan has been reluctant to diversify its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and to seek higher returns by making active investments, unlike many other countries which have been recently investing a portion of its reserves in stocks and other high-risk high-return assets.   \par \par \uc2 Vice Finance Minister Hiroki Tsuda said on Thursday that there was no change in that stance.   \par \par \uc2 His comments came after a member of the government's top economic advisory body called for setting up a new fund to actively manage a small part of external reserves.   \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/RESERVES (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071005e3a500036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
