\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Japan Nukaga: Want BOJ Policy To Be Consistent With Govt's   \b0\par\par\uc2 763 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2007\par 11:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--New Japanese Finance Minister Fukushiro Nukaga said Wednesday the economy has yet to escape deflation, and he urged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to steer monetary policy in a way that conforms to the government's economic policy.   \par \par \uc2 Nukaga, who was named by Prime Minister Shinzo Abe to head the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Monday's Cabinet reshuffle, also said it is important for the government to try to keep exchange rates stable. And he signaled opposition to the idea of more actively investing Japan's massive foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for better returns - a step analysts say could increase \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility.   \par \par \uc2 Nukaga's cautious assessment of the Japanese economy indicates that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  under his stewardship may not be friendly to the Bank of Japan, which is believed to favor raising interest rates from the current low levels.   \par \par \uc2 'We are not at the stage where we can say we're completely out of deflation,' Nukaga said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires and other media organizations. 'Japan's economy today isn't on a full-fledged (growth) path. We must work to put it on an upward trend.'   \par \par \uc2 Though Nukaga repeated the government's official line that monetary policy is under the jurisdiction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said: 'In terms of economic policies, it's desirable that the Bank of Japan will make policy in a way that is rational and consistent (with the government's policy) and stabilize the overall economy.'   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month left its target for key short-term rates unchanged at 0.50%, and analysts expect it may stand pat next month as well. But BOJ Gov. Toshihiko Fukui, at a news conference following the rate decision, hinted the bank will continue its campaign of gradual rate hikes once global market turbulence caused by problems in the U.S. subprime mortgage market passes without serious damage to Japan's economy.   \par \par \uc2 Natural For Government To Try To Curb \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Volatility   \par \par \uc2 In what is his first official commentary on Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, Nukaga indicated he won't rule out the possibility of Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stabilize the yen's exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 'I'm in charge of (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy), so I would better not comment' on exchange rates, Nukaga said. 'But in principle, for the economy to continue stable growth, it's natural for a government to make efforts to ensure there won't be any excessive exchange-rate volatility.'   \par \par \uc2 Still, the majority of market participants believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than three years, will stay out of the markets unless the yen's rise seriously threatens to derail the country's economic pickup.   \par \par \uc2 Commenting on Japan's more than $900 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stockpiles, Nukaga said that 'the primary aim of holding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is to use them to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'   \par \par \uc2 'I would like to be cautious in thinking about (foreign reserve) management that involves risks.'   \par \par \uc2 His remarks came after Abe's Cabinet reshuffle forced advocates of active reserve management out of the government, such as former Bank Minister Yuji Yamamoto and Chief Cabinet Secretary Yasuhisa Shiozaki. Nukaga's stance indicates the chances of any major changes in Japan's reserve policy in the near future are slim.   \par \par \uc2 Nukaga Wednesday also said the world's financial markets are 'becoming relatively stable' after several weeks of turbulence caused by fallout from surging defaults on U.S. subprime mortgages.   \par \par \uc2 'I don't think (recent market movements) will have any big impact on Japan's economy.'   \par \par \uc2 Nukaga largely sidestepped questions on whether he is for or against raising the country's consumption tax rate, currently at 5%.   \par \par \uc2 While he acknowledged that Japan needs new sources of tax revenues to cover its ballooning social security payments, Nukaga didn't clarify his position, saying, 'I would like (issues about the consumption tax) to be discussed and would like to hear a conclusion.'   \par \par \uc2 Responding to a question on who he thinks should succeed BOJ Gov. Fukui when the governor's term runs out in March, Nukaga said it could be a former bureaucrat, a private-sector expert or just 'anyone who is appropriate at the given time and under given conditions.'   \par \par \uc2 In recent months, top officials of Japan's opposition parties indicated they may be opposed if the ruling coalition tries to replace Fukui with BOJ Deputy Gov. Toshiro Muto. -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020070829e38t0053d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
