\par\par \b \uc2 No Big Changes Seen From Japan's New Finance Minister Nukaga   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kenneth McCallum   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2007\par 10:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's new finance minister, Fukushiro Nukaga, is a veteran lawmaker in the ruling party with ministerial experience and a checkered political career. He's an unknown quantity when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fiscal policy, but isn't expected to make radical changes.   \par \par \uc2 As part of a Cabinet reshuffle Monday in the wake of a big defeat in elections last month, Prime Minister Shinzo Abe named Nukaga to replace Koji Omi as the head of the powerful \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sets Japan's fiscal, tax and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.   \par \par \uc2 Nukaga is an influential member of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party who was first elected to the Lower House of Parliament in 1983 and has held such key posts as defense minister, economy minister and policy head of the LDP.   \par \par \uc2 But the 63-year old politician has had to resign from top posts twice over scandals.   \par \par \uc2 In 1998, Nukaga quit as defense chief after an opposition-controlled upper chamber passed a censure motion against him over a defense procurement scandal. He stepped down in 2001 after two months as economy chief, following revelations that he had accepted donations - later returned - from a scandal-tainted firm.   \par \par \uc2 Some political insiders say opposition parties could use Nukaga's past as ammunition to attack Prime Minister Abe's weakened administration. The biggest opposition group, the Democratic Party of Japan, took over control of the Upper House in July's elections, giving it a platform from which to attack the ruling coalition.   \par \par \uc2 Nukaga was likely picked as finance minister partly because he's a member of a major LDP faction, and Abe needed the faction's support to continue to run things as prime minister after July's election disaster.   \par \par \uc2 As is often the case for Japanese Cabinet picks, Nukaga doesn't have many clear qualifications for running the ministry he's been named to head. Besides serving briefly as economy minister, he's worked at the trade ministry and has been the deputy head of the LDP's tax panel. But he hasn't been involved much in policy-making at the Finance Ministry, considered Japan's most powerful bureaucracy. But some political insiders say he works well with MOF officials.   \par \par \uc2 Still, market players don't expect Nukaga to stray too far from his predecessors' stances in setting the ministry's policy. The yen and Japanese government bonds showed essentially no reaction to news of Nukaga's selection as finance chief.   \par \par \uc2 "Japanese people basically think that the government's (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fiscal) policy won't change so much even after the reshuffle," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at major Japanese bank.   \par \par \uc2 Like Omi before him, on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy Nukaga might limit himself to warning against sharp moves in the yen's exchange rates, without actually ordering market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless moves get out of hand. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since 2004.   \par \par \uc2 He might also ask the Bank of Japan to be cautious in deciding whether to lift interest rates - in effect, putting pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stand pat on monetary policy.   \par \par \uc2 "The government is very supportive of the business world and against a BOJ interest-rate hike," Shinsei Securities senior analyst Yasuhiro Matsumoto says. Their view is that the BOJ "should retain the current policy on interest rates for the time being, until subprime concerns are resolved."   \par \par \uc2 As economy minister in 2000, Nukaga several times called on Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its super-easy policy. But such declarations by top government economic policymakers weren't unusual in those days of financial crisis and deep economic slump in Japan.   \par \par \uc2 The BOJ's policy board left its key overnight rate unchanged Thursday, judging that monetary policy shouldn't be tightened at a time of global credit market jitters. Its next meeting is Sept. 18-19, and markets only see about a one-third chance of the BOJ raising rates then.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Kenneth McCallum, Dow Jones Newswires, 813-5255-2929; ken.mccallum@dowjones.com    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (With contributions by James Simms and Michael S. Arnold.)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by William Mallard [ 27-08-07 0803GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070827e38r000c2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
