\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day   \b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2007\par 14:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJA: ASIAN SHARES CONTINUE FALL ON US SUB-PRIME WOES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fallout from U.S. sub-prime mortgage crisis hammered Japan's stock market Friday, leading to the largest single-day loss there since the Sept. 11, 2001, terrorist attacks in the U.S. (published 0656 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN GOVT: MARKET TURBULENCE WON'T AFFECT ECONOMY    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The outlook for the German economy remains strong despite ongoing market turbulence, a government spokesman said Friday. (published 0636 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN FIN MIN: NO ONE SHOULD QUESTION ECB INDEPENDENCE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Finance Minister Peer Steinbrueck Friday came to the defense of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , saying its independence is crucial to deliver price stability. (published 0607 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =ASIA-PACIFIC CENTRAL BANKS SEEK TO KEEP MARKETS LIQUID    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan pumped more cash into the money market Friday while Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it's ready to inject liquidity again, underscoring that Asia-Pacific central banks remain determined to fight off the credit crisis engulfing global financial markets. (published 1132 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WTO CHIEF:POOR NATIONS WILL GET GOOD CONCESSIONS FROM RICH ONES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The World Trade Organization's top official predicted Friday that poor nations will win satisfactory concessions from rich ones by the end of talks on a new global trade accord. (published 0718 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HURRICANE DEAN STRIKES E CARIBBEAN ISLES; WINDS AT 100 MPH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Powerful Hurricane Dean tore through the eastern Caribbean early Friday, hitting the islands of St. Lucia and Martinique. (published 0725 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ========== \par  \par =MIDDAY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : DOLLAR, YEN BACK DOWN A LITTLE    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A recovery in some European stocks helped both the dollar and the yen to back down in Europe Friday. (published 0551 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =STREET SAVVY:10% DROP FEELS BAD? HISTORY SHOWS MORE TO COME    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crossing 13000 on the Dow sure was a lot more fun the first time around. (published 0733 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =INSINGER DE BEAUFORT: US STRATEGY BEHIND MARKET WOES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nobody should have thought the withdrawal of the 'Greenspan put' would be painless. (published 0728 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =BROKERS TO SEE SOME CREDIT IMPACT, BUT ARE WELL FUNDED:FITCH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors in U.S. brokerage firms can expect lower earnings in the future, but the firms are well funded and have sufficient capacity to absorb losses from marking their assets to market, a report by Fitch said Thursday. (published 0704 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FOCUS: FORINT TOPS LOSERS IN CTRL EUROPE AS CARRY UNWINDS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The turmoil rocking global financial markets has swept into Eastern Europe, where high-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are taking hits, especially the Hungarian forint. (published 0712 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =FOCUS:UK FUND MANAGERS FAVOR PROPERTY DESPITE DOWNTURN    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Major investors are maintaining their holdings in the U.K. property market saying they are strong investments in tough times even as shares continue to slide among listed property companies. (published 0725 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =KEY EURO-ZONE DATA:GERMAN ZEW TO SLUMP ON GLOBAL MKT JITTERS    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The German ZEW economic sentiment survey is expected to slump in August as the current global credit and capital market jitters weigh on business sentiment. (published 0740 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =YEN SKYROCKETS, BUT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  UNLIKELY TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - YET    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen has skyrocketed on global financial turmoil, posting its biggest one-day spike in nearly a decade against the dollar, but \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's authorities don't look likely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market - at least for now. (published 0501 ET)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =ASSET CLASS: CHINA, NOT A BANKER TO TUSSLE WITH    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. is China's problem. It was John Paul Getty who said that if you owe the bank $100, that's your problem. If you owe the bank $100 million, that's the bank's problem. (published 0529 ET) [ 17-08-07 1200GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070817e38h000k8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
