\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:Asia Central Bks Inject Cash; Play Dn Liquidity Worry   \b0\par\par\uc2 871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2007\par 09:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , money market rates.)   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Central banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Australia and Singapore leapt into the fray to calm jittery financial markets Friday, injecting cash into their local money markets in an echo of overnight action by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Federal Reserve.   \par \par \uc2 The authorities in Malaysia, the Philippines and Indonesia meanwhile \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 However, central bankers and government officials across the region were circumspect about the prospects for a credit crunch here, likely for fear of feeding the panic that has arisen out of the snowballing U.S. subprime crisis. Concerns about subprime have prompted a flight to highly rated assets and made it more difficult for investors to access ever more cautious markets.   \par \par \uc2 "Any impact on the Philippines will be largely indirect, mainly in the form of risk aversion," said Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas Governor Amando Tetangco said.   \par \par \uc2 "There is sufficient domestic liquidity," he added.   \par \par \uc2 In Korea, where the finance ministry has just wrapped up a meeting with officials from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Financial Supervisory Commission and commercial banks to discuss the global market volatility, Vice Finance Minister Lim Young-Rok warned the impact from the subprime may continue for some time but that sufficient liquidity meant the bank wasn't planning to inject any fresh money into the market.   \par \par \uc2 Asia broadly has been raising interest rates of late to slow hefty liquidity growth, with South Korea and Australia tightening policy earlier this week and regional economic giants China and Japan expected to follow suit in coming weeks or months.   \par \par \uc2 Speculation will now turn to whether and how much the snowballing credit market fallout will change the Asian rate cycle or damage local economies and whether governments and central banks are ready to act.   \par \par \uc2 "Suddenly, central banks have changed their tune from a wait-and-see stance on the subprime issue to a move to stabilize the financial system,"  \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  analyst Masuhisa Kobayashi wrote in a research note Friday.   \par \par \uc2 He said that the start of action could either mean that the credit market and financial system have destabilized so much that central banks had to act or that the action was meant to prevent such a situation, and things should calm down going forward.   \par \par \uc2 "The market seems to have taken the former, more pessimistic view," he added.   \par \par \uc2 For their part, Japanese government officials played down worries, with economy minister Hiroko Ota saying the U.S. subprime issue would wreak little damage on the Japanese economy and that Japanese monetary policy would be based on the state of the local economy and prices.   \par \par \uc2 Still,  \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said the chances of a rate hike at the BOJ meeting later this month had fallen to 37% Friday from 64% Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan injected Y1 trillion into the local money market Friday after the overnight call rate rose above the bank's target rate of 0.5% for overnight loans. Sharp gyrations followed but by mid afternoon the overnight rate was quoted at 0.5049%.   \par \par \uc2 Banks weren't facing fund-raising problems but wanted to preemptively ensure as much funding as possible amid growing credit anxiety, said one senior money trader in Tokyo. He added that banks that borrowed money through the BOJ's injection were holding the funds, encouraging other banks to raise their bid rates.   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of Australia injected A$4.95 billion of liquidity into Australia's financial system, double the A$2.44 billion needed.   \par \par \uc2 Spokesmen for both played down the actions as part of normal dealing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore also injected S$1.5 billion into the market in what they said was a symbolic gesture, coming as it did shortly after Deputy Managing Director Ong Chong Tee said the MAS was ready to step in to offer liquidity if necessary.   \par \par \uc2 Following the injection, the overnight deposit rate jumped to 2.75% before falling to 2.56% in mid afternoon. That was still higher than 2.25% at the start of the day.   \par \par \uc2 On the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, where high-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were slammed by the rush to safe-haven units, dealers said the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold US$3 million at MYR3.48 to smooth the pair's gains and that the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also in the market supporting the rupiah.   \par \par \uc2 Traders in Manila said the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was selling U.S. dollars at PHP45.75 to infuse liquidity into a market due to heavy U.S. dollar buying from oil firms and banks.   \par \par \uc2 The action across Asia followed decisions by the ECB and the Federal Reserve to inject EUR94.84 billion and $24 billion respectively into their markets.   \par \par \uc2 That was sparked by spiking overnight rates after  \uc2 BNP Paribas SA\uc2 , France's biggest bank, said Thursday it had stopped withdrawals from three investment funds due to volatility in the U.S. asset-backed securities market.   \par \par \uc2 Subsequent market jitters overnight sent a shockwave through move Asian markets Friday.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Karen Lane, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415-4043; karen.lane@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-08-07 0713GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070810e38a000ro\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
