\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-POLL-Many firms expect  \highlight22\uc2 Japan forex intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shigeo Kodama   \par \uc2 891 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juli 2007\par 01:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds analyst comments, background)   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 19 (Reuters) - Nearly half of major Japanese companies expect the government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the yen if it falls further, a Reuters poll showed on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The survey, part of the Reuters Tankan, a monthly survey of leading Japanese companies, showed that less than a fifth of Japanese companies want the authorities to take action on the yen, while about a quarter think they should stay out of the markets.   \par \par \uc2 "It depends on the pace (of yen falls), but I think the authorities would be very cautious about stepping into the market to buy yen," said Kenichiro Yoshida, a senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute.   \par \par \uc2 Most Japanese companies said the yen's decline so far would have a limited impact on their profit outlook. The yen has been falling largely because of Japan's low interest rates, by far the lowest among major economies.   \par \par \uc2 The yen's trade-weighted value hit a 22-year low last month, and some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have started to wonder whether it may soon reach what the Japanese government sees as a pain threshold.   \par \par \uc2 The yen's depreciation makes imports more costly and could hurt consumer sentiment by lifting prices of imported goods such as gasoline, although it also benefits the country's exporters by boosting their competitiveness.   \par \par \uc2 Of the 225 companies that responded to the survey, conducted June 28-July 13, 44 percent saw Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets, while 56 percent expected no such an action.   \par \par \uc2 But many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near future as current yen selling has not led to foreign investors dumping Japanese assets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are not seen as sufficiently excessive to warrant a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "The authorities may start to issue verbal warnings if the dollar rises above 125 yen and approaches 130 yen," Yoshida said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar changed hands at around 122 yen on Thursday, off its 4-1/2-year high of 124.14 hit in June.   \par \par \uc2 Japan has stayed out of the market since March 2004, when it ended a massive yen-selling spree, the longest Tokyo has gone without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on current records dating back to 1991.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets from late 1997 to mid-1998 when the country was in a financial crisis and foreign investors were dumping Japanese assets.   \par \par \uc2 Among companies that expect yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , about a quarter said the government may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar rises above 125 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Another 29 percent expect Tokyo to step in if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises above 128 yen. The rest think Japan will wait until the dollar rises above 130 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Over the past year, the yen has fallen about 9 percent against the dollar. It hit a record low of 168.95 yen against the euro and a 16-year low of 250.49 yen against sterling earlier this month.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have dropped few hints on the authorities' intentions on the yen, repeating that the policy is not to target specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels and the ministry wants exchange rates to reflect economic fundamentals.   \par \par \uc2 Most Japanese companies said the yen's fall so far would have little impact on their profits. Eighty-six percent of companies said the yen's decline would have no impact on their earnings in the current financial year to March.   \par \par \uc2 Only 3 percent said the yen would hurt their profits, while 11 percent said it would boost their bottom line.   \par \par \uc2 Perhaps reflecting the limited impact on corporate earnings, the poll also showed limited support for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  among Japanese companies.   \par \par \uc2 Only 18 percent of Japanese companies said the government should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to block the yen's fall, while 23 percent said such manoeuvring is not necessary.   \par \par \uc2 But 58 percent of respondents said they did not care whether the government buys yen in the market.   \par \par \uc2 The following is a table of the results:   \par \par \uc2 Q. How does the yen's fall affect your profit outlook for this financial year? (percentage of respondents)    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                                 TOTAL    MANUF  NON-MANUF\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par  Boosts profits more than 20 pct     0        0       0\par  Boosts profits 5-20 pct             1        2       0\par  Boosts profits less than 5 pct     10       14       6\par  No impact                          86       80      92\par  Cuts profits less than 5 pct        3        3       2\par  Cuts profits 5-20 pct               0        0       0\par  Cuts profits more than 20 pct       0        0       0\par  Q. Do you expect the government to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the yen falls\par further? (percentage of respondents)\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                                 TOTAL    MANUF NON-MANUF\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par  Yes                                44       44       43\par  No                                 56       56       57\par  Q. (question to those who said yes to the question above)\par  At what dollar/yen level, do you think the government will\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ? (percentage of respondents)\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par   Dollar/yen                       TOTAL    MANUF NON-MANUF\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par  Above 125 yen                      26       21       30\par  Above 128 yen                      29       31       26\par  Above 130 yen                      37       42       30\par  Above 135 yen                       9        6       13\par  Above 140 yen                       0        0        0\par  Q. Do you want the government to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets\par to stop further slides in the yen?\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                                 TOTAL    MANUF NON-MANUF\par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par  Yes                                18       19        18\par  No                                 23       24        22\par  Don't care                         58       57        60\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-TANKAN/YEN (POLL) (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0