\par\par \b \uc2 Japan MOF Vice Fin Min Tsuda: Monitoring  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Mkts Carefully   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 879 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2007\par 10:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Japanese Finance Ministry's new top bureaucrat said the ministry is monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets "carefully," but was tight-lipped about whether Tokyo would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help a trailing yen.   \par \par \uc2 Hiroki Tsuda, a 58-year-old career bureaucrat who officially became administrative vice finance minister Tuesday, also said the ministry has no plans to start investing the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stockpiles more actively for better returns.   \par \par \uc2 Tsuda has taken up the reins from his predecessor, Hideto Fujii, at a sensitive time. Inspired by China, South Korea and other Asian nations diversifying part of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, some Japanese lawmakers are urging the Finance Ministry to follow suit. Japanese business leaders are meanwhile ringing alarm bells about the yen's persistent decline against other key \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The tone of Tsuda's comments suggests the ministry is uncomfortable with the falling yen, but still doesn't see the need to force the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher through market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last conducted yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June 1998 when the dollar rose to a high of Y146.73.   \par \par \uc2 "We're monitoring developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets carefully," Tsuda said in a recent interview with Dow Jones Newswires. "Our basic stance is that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect economic fundamentals, and in addition to that, Japan's economy is doing fine."   \par \par \uc2 Tsuda declined to comment on whether the yen's broad, continuous declines over the past two and a half years are in line with Japan's recovering economy.   \par \par \uc2 "We are, on a routine basis, carefully looking at the relationship between the economy and exchange rates, and having debates about them. But we shouldn't make any comments on that," Tsuda said.   \par \par \uc2 "We won't comment on anything about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " either, he added.   \par \par \uc2 The euro Monday rose to Y168.55 versus the yen, the highest level ever since Europe launched the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1999. The U.S. dollar last month hit a four-and-a-half-year high of Y124.14. At 0600 GMT Tuesday, the dollar stood at Y123.41 and the euro at Y167.92 on EBS.   \par \par \uc2 Tsuda's new position is the most powerful post for Finance Ministry bureaucrats, in charge of supervising changing tax codes, outlining the national budget and managing the nation's huge public debt.   \par \par \uc2 Although he doesn't directly oversee foreign affairs, such as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which are handled by the vice finance minister for international affairs, Tsuda's remarks carry weight because of his highest ranking post.   \par \par \uc2 Also, the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy makers usually pre-write \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments for administrative vice finance ministers.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Reserves Must Be In Safe, Liquid Assets \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tsuda also sounded cautious about diversifying the country's $913-billion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves - a step analysts say could have a big impact on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets as well as the U.S. bond market.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We have no plans to make any big changes to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  makeup of the reserves," Tsuda said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The important thing is that (reserves) are held in safe assets and are sufficiently liquid (for the ministry) to employ anytime. Those are the two main principles...I think those principles haven't been changed, and within those rules, we should discuss how we may be able to increase returns."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan is believed to hold most of its reserves - the world's second largest after China's - in U.S. government bonds and dollar-denominated deposits. Tsuda's stance signals that the ministry has no intention to sell U.S. securities and dollars for more profitable assets elsewhere.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In handling the reserves, "we need to avoid causing any adverse developments that could disturb the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets or other countries' financial markets," Tsuda said. "That's something we need to be very mindful of."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I understand that there are such arguments" that Tokyo should at least diversify interest income earned on the reserves, Tsuda said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And "we're also studying various things," such as how other countries are running their reserves.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But I believe we are to think about things based on the (two) major principles," he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He hasn't heard of any plans of the MOF opening talks on the issue with other ministries, Tsuda added.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commenting on Japan's economy, Tsuda said it is "recovering stably," pointing to rising corporate earnings and business investment and a pickup in consumer spending.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But in a sign that he doesn't think the time is ripe for raising interest rates, Tsuda urged the Bank of Japan to run monetary policy in a way that supports growth.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We would like monetary policy authorities to support economic recovery from the monetary side," Tsuda said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's up to the Bank of Japan to decide how to operate monetary policy," as the Japanese laws governing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dictate, he said. But those laws also say the government and the BOJ should stick to the same policy goals "as much as they can," so "I really want (the bank) to support economy from the monetary policy side."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mayuko Ota [ 10-07-07 0800GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070710e37a000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
