\par\par \b \uc2 EX-MOF Sakakibara: Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Stance Change Unlikely   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 818 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2007\par 09:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's newly appointed vice finance minister for international affairs is unlikely to try to force the yen higher because the country's low interest rates present too great of a barrier, a former Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  point person said Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 "Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance probably won't change" for the time being, Eisuke Sakakibara, who served as vice finance minister for international affairs between 1997-1999, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.   \par \par \uc2 "That's because, unless interest rate differentials (between \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other advanced nations) narrow, (yen-buying) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have little impact," he said.   \par \par \uc2 His comments came after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted a regular reshuffling of senior positions earlier this week.   \par \par \uc2 Naoyuki Shinohara, a 54-year-old elite MOF bureaucrat and an expert on international finance, will be the next to assume the top post for bureaucrats handling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from July 10.   \par \par \uc2 Shinohara's policy principles are little known in part because he has seldom spoken in public.   \par \par \uc2 But Sakakibara expects that Tokyo, which hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for three years even as the yen logged multiyear lows against various other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will continue its hands-off approach under Shinohara.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's target for the overnight call rate, charged on overnight loans between banks, stands at 0.50%. That's far below the U.S. policy rate of 5.25% or the European rate of 4.00%.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's relatively low rates, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance of lifting them only gradually, have prompted investors to engage in yen-carry trades, in which they sell borrowed yen to invest in more profitable assets outside the nation.   \par \par \uc2 "For the yen to turn around, the BOJ must quicken the pace of interest-rate increases" to make yen-carry trades less profitable, Sakakibara said.   \par \par \uc2 "If the BOJ started raising interest rates at a faster pace, then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be very effective. But if you \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the BOJ's stance is unchanged, you would only provide (market participants) with a very good chance to buy (the dollar against the yen cheaply)."   \par \par \uc2 "Players like Japanese individuals and institutional investors would certainly buy (the dollar) if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled it lower to Y120 or so" against the yen as they're hungry for higher interest in the U.S., he said. "That's a sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  you should never conduct."   \par \par \uc2 Around 0740 GMT Thursday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was changing hands at Y122.66 versus the yen.   \par \par \uc2 Yen-carry trades will probably remain popular even if the BOJ gradually raises rates to 1.00% by the end of the year, given that many players are well aware of such prospects but are still selling the yen, Sakakibara said. Therefore, the dollar is likely to stay in a range of Y115.00 to Y125.00 by the year-end, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara is known as Mr. Yen for his ability during his tenure at the MOF to move the exchange rate with his comments. He is currently director of Waseda University's Indian economic studies institute.   \par \par \uc2 Recent Japan Talks On \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserves Not Appropriate   \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara meanwhile advised Japanese lawmakers not to openly discuss the idea of more actively investing the county's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to increase returns.   \par \par \uc2 "The logic may be right, but that's something you shouldn't talk about openly because doing so could have a significant impact on the markets" given the huge size of Japan's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stockpiles, Sakakibara said. "For example, if Japan decided to diversify its dollar-denominated assets (included in the reserves) into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that would certainly move the markets."   \par \par \uc2 Financial Services Minister Yuji Yamamoto has recently hinted that an economic policy-making panel headed by Prime Minister Shinzo Abe might discuss a review of how Japan manages its $911-billion reserves, the world's second largest after China.   \par \par \uc2 Yamamoto floated that idea earlier this week when he visited Singapore, which is known for its active handling of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings.   \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara noted that Singapore is "a small country, and even if Singapore does something, that wouldn't move the markets."   \par \par \uc2 In stark contrast, changes in the reserve portfolios of Japan or China could have "a significant impact" on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Sakakibara said. Given that Tokyo is believed to hold most of its reserves in U.S. government bonds, "the impact would be felt in the U.S. bond market" as well, he said.   \par \par \uc2 "If (Japanese lawmakers) want to start active management, they must work out the details of how to do it and what strategies to employ. Caution is the most important thing here," Sakakibara said. "Making comments without hammering out any details is nonsense. That's amateurish."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-07-07 0746GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070705e375000iq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
