\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves edge up on interest rate income   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa   \par \uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2007\par 01:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Adds details, background, byline)   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 6 (Reuters) - Japan's foreign reserves, the world's second-largest, rose to $913.572 billion at the end of June, thanks to interest rate income from deposits and bonds held in the reserves, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  They were up from $911.137 billion at the end of May but shy of the record $915.623 billion hit at the end of April.   \par \par \uc2  A rise in the euro, which boosted the dollar value of euro-denominated reserves, contributed to the increase, a ministry official told reporters.   \par \par \uc2  The euro traded at $1.3542 at the end of June, up from $1.3453 a month earlier, the official said.   \par \par \uc2  The reserves at the end of June included $768.407 billion in securities and $124.354 billion in deposits. The rest includes IMF reserve positions, special drawing rights and gold.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's reserves are second only to those of China, which has $1.2 trillion in external reserves.   \par \par \uc2  Russia ranks third, followed by Taiwan and South Korea, the official said.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves ballooned after yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounting to a record 20 trillion yen ($162.7 billion) in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of 2004, as Tokyo tried to keep a rapid rise in the yen from derailing a then-fragile economic recovery and accelerating deflation.   \par \par \uc2  Tokyo has kept out of the market since then, but the reserves have continued to grow steadily, partly on interest rate income.   \par \par \uc2  The government does not disclose the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdown of the external reserves. But historical data on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has mostly taken the form of dollar buying, suggests most of Tokyo's hefty reserves are in dollars.   \par \par \uc2  The MOF, which is in charge of foreign reserves management, has said it has no plans to diversify the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  make-up.   \par \par \uc2  It is also cautious about the idea of setting up a new public agency to more actively manage foreign reserves, although China's decision to set up an investment fund to manage part of its massive reserves has prompted some lawmakers to question whether Japan is doing enough.   \par \par \uc2  The MOF's official guideline says attention should be paid to safety and liquidity in managing the nation's reserves, although under those constraints the government will pursue profitability.   \par \par \uc2  Over the past few years, the MOF has been diversifying the types of dollar-denominated assets in the reserves and has also shifted to a more active management approach from its traditional buy-and-hold policy.   \par \par \uc2  But calls for improvement in the system persist.   \par \par \uc2  Takatoshi Ito, a member of the government's top economic council, told Reuters on Thursday that Japan should more effectively manage public money, including its foreign reserves, and one idea is to set up a new fund to manage interest rate income from foreign assets held in the reserves. [ID:nT5033]   \par \par \uc2  Currently, short-term government paper is issued to balance a special budget account that holds Japan's foreign reserves when the asset side of the balance sheet increases due to interest rate income from deposits and securities held in the reserves.   \par \par \uc2  Setting up a fund for at least the interest rate income could avoid the need for extra issuance of public debt, Ito said.   \par \par \uc2  Ito visited Singapore with Financial Services Minister Yuji Yamamoto this week and their itinerary included the  \uc2 Government of Singapore Investment Corp.\uc2  (GIC), which invests the city state's foreign reserves and budget surplus in real estate and other assets to seek higher returns.  Yamamoto said after the trip that the government could learn from other countries' examples and keep reviewing what Japan can and should do to more effectively manage public funds, including pension funds and foreign reserves.   \par \par \uc2  Yamamoto said at a news conference on Friday that he had told Prime Minister Shinzo Abe the management of public funds such as pension funds needed to be improved.   \par \par \uc2   * Click on [ID:nT78580] for a table of main figures and [ID:nT343251] for a factbox on Japan's reserves management.        \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-RESERVES/(UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|J|ABN|M|E|RBN|U|D|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070705e3750021y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
