\par\par \b \uc2 No econ reason for BOJ to wait until Aug to hike   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa and Yuko Yoshikawa   \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2007\par 08:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 4 (Reuters) - There is no economic reason for the Bank of Japan to hold off from raising interest rates until August or later given steady growth at home and improvement in the global economy, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Eiji Hirano, who served as the BOJ's assistant governor until last June, also told Reuters in an interview that the yen's recent falls broadly reflect economic fundamentals and he saw no need for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy yen.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't understand why most analysts are ruling out a rate hike in July," said Hirano, currently executive vice president at  \uc2 Toyota Financial Services Corporation\uc2 , citing market players' expectations of a BOJ rate hike as early as August.   \par \par \uc2 "I see no economic grounds for the BOJ to wait until August. We have an upper house election (in late July) and you can get more data by waiting another month, but we already had the BOJ's tankan corporate survey, and overseas economies are doing better than several months ago," he added.   \par \par \uc2 But Hirano said he had not talked with any member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nine-member policy board recently on this matter.   \par \par \uc2 Many investors expect the BOJ to lift the overnight call rate target by a quarter percentage point to 0.75 percent in August or September. The BOJ's policy board meets next on July 11-12.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's economy is now heavily affected by globalisation, and emerging inflationary pressure around the world will eventually push up prices here, with a weak yen likely adding to the upward inflationary impetus, Hirano said.   \par \par \uc2 "People are now looking at consumer prices through a magnifying glass, with foolish discussions like how mobile phone charges pushed them down and so on, getting more nearsighted," Hirano said.   \par \par \uc2 "But if you look at the broader picture, I think there is a risk that inflation may heighten in Japan over the next year."   \par \par \uc2 Hirano said the yen's recent fall reflected interest rate differentials between Japan and other nations and the relatively gloomier long-term economic outlook for Japan, which faces challenges such as an ageing society.   \par \par \uc2 Individual investors, as a result, are seeking better returns by buying foreign assets, pushing down the yen, he added.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't think professional speculators are simply driving the yen weakness. Rather, the circle of people who are behind the yen's decline has spread wider. In that sense, the market is not distorted," Hirano said.   \par \par \uc2 During June, the yen depreciated across the board. The euro hit its highest levels against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since its 1999 launch, while the dollar posted a 4 1/2-year peak of 124.14 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Hirano, who had focused on international monetary issues while serving as a BOJ assistant governor, noted that low volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has also boosted carry trades, in which traders use low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the yen as cheap funds to buy assets offering higher returns elsewhere.   \par \par \uc2 If volatility increases for reasons such as heightening uncertainty over the global economy, there may be some unwinding of yen carry trades, but a resulting rise in the yen would likely be a one-off phenomenon, not a lasting trend, Hirano said.   \par \par \uc2 He said there was no need for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is in charge of the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop the yen's fall as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has broadly been moving in line with economic fundamentals.   \par \par \uc2 He noted that the situation is different from 1998 when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy yen. The economy was then on the verge of collapse and there was a bout of "Japan selling", he added.   \par \par \uc2 "The yen is not being pushed down by speculators, either. There are no grounds for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ECONOMY/HIRANO (INTERVIEW)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070707e37400037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
