\par\par \b \uc2 NEWSMAKER-Japan picks int'l policy veteran as new  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  tsar   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Nishikawa   \par \uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2007\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 3 (Reuters) - Naoyuki Shinohara, Japan's newly appointed top financial diplomat, is known as a pragmatist who doesn't mince words, but little is known about his views on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , one of the most crucial parts of his job.   \par \par \uc2 Shinohara will replace Hiroshi Watanabe, Japan's high-profile vice finance minister for international affairs, on July 10 and will take charge of the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, working directly with Finance Minister Koji Omi.   \par \par \uc2 His appointment comes at a time when traders are wary about the prospect that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop the yen's decline or change its policy on the makeup of its $911 billion of foreign reserves.   \par \par \uc2 Shinohara, head of the international bureau at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , once worked in the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  division, where he focused on managing Japan's reserves. That was from 1983 to 1985, just before the Plaza Accord when leading countries came together to push down the value of the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 For the past few years, the Princeton-educated bureaucrat has worked under Watanabe, who was seen as more opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than his predecessors.   \par \par \uc2 Little is known about Shinohara's own views on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however. He has not spoken much in public, and his few comments have basically been in line with the ministry's official stance.   \par \par \uc2 Disclosure of the impending job change by a government source last week had lifted the yen slightly, on the view that Watanabe's departure could signal a more proactive approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yen's real trade-weighted value hit a fresh 22-year low in June.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have stayed out of the market since March 2004, when they ended a massive yen-selling spree. The hands-off stretch marks the longest time on record that Tokyo has gone without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets from late 1997 to mid-1998 when the country was in a financial crisis and foreign investors were dumping Japanese assets.   \par \par \uc2 But analysts are now doubtful of a drastic policy shift.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't expect a big new initiative from him," said Tomoko Fujii, head of economics and strategy for  \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 "We will of course be watching what he may say on reserves management policy. But everyone knows any hint of changing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  make-up of Japan's massive foreign reserves could roil the market, so I don't expect a change in Japan's policy because of him," she added.   \par \par \uc2 After Omi signalled some wariness about the yen's weakness over the past few weeks, traders remain sensitive to any comments about the yen from Japanese authorities.   \par \par \uc2 Recent talk about Japan's Asian neighbours seeking to more actively manage their foreign reserves has prompted some policymakers to ask whether Japan should do the same.   \par \par \uc2 One of Shinohara's challenges in his new job will be hosting the G7/G8 finance ministers' meetings in Japan next year.   \par \par \uc2 Shinohara joined the MOF in 1975 as a graduate of the high-profile University of Tokyo. As he climbed the ladder at the ministry's international bureau, he made contacts with officials from Group of Seven industrialised nations by serving as a senior official, called G7 deputies' deputy, who made preparations behind the scene for meetings of the group's finance ministers.   \par \par \uc2 Shinohara, who talks in subdued tones, is also known to be keen on Asian issues.   \par \par \uc2 He headed the ministry's international organisation division that deals with issues related to the G7 and the International Monetary Fund in 1996-97, just before Asian financial crisis hit the region.   \par \par \uc2 After a year at the international development policy division, he was seconded to the Asian Development Bank as an executive director in 1998.   \par \par \uc2 Besides his experience in the ministry's budget and banking bureaus, Shinohara has worked as a negotiator on the world stage, including Japan-U.S. economic partnership talks and the Uruguay Round of trade talks.   \par \par \uc2 It is unclear whether Shinohara will follow the lead of Watanabe and hold briefings for reporters regularly during his tenure as head of the international bureau as well as vice finance minister.   \par \par \uc2 *Click [ID:nT302282] for main story on the ministry's personnel reshuffle.   \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/SHINOHARA (NEWSMAKER)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070703e373000r4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
